client_key                 \-\-client\-key
sidecar (bool)             \-\-sidecar
\(em\(em cache \(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
persistent                 \-\-persistent
persistent_interval        \-\-persistent\-interval     5m, 30s, 1h
maxmemory                  \-\-maxmemory     256M, or a byte count
eviction                   \-\-eviction
resp (bool)                \-\-resp
replicate                  \-\-replicate
cluster (bool)             \-\-cluster
.fi
.PP
A key this list does not name is refused, naming it, rather than dropped in
silence: a misspelled
.B http_cache
used to configure nothing while the runtime came up looking correct. A value of
the wrong shape is refused the same way, and so is a value containing
whitespace \(em the daemon's command line has no quoting, so a password with a
space in it would arrive as two arguments.
.PP
A key the daemon then refuses on that type \(em
.B \-\-client\-ca
on a cleartext proxy, say \(em is refused by the daemon, in the daemon's own
words, under the name of the runtime it belongs to. The remaining entries are
still applied, and the exit code is 1.
.PP
.B daemon_process
was in this list and is gone with the flag it emitted. A configuration file
still carrying it is refused, naming the line, rather than accepted with the key
quietly dropped \(em which would leave an operator believing a runtime is in the
daemon while it is not.
.PP
Nothing replaces it. The whole cross\-runtime API works in a worker:
.BR socketley.send ,
.BR .list ,
.B .get
and
.B socketley.cluster
are answered from the replica map the daemon pushes;
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop
and
.B .remove
are asked of the daemon and wait for its answer; and
.B socketley.pipe
and
.B self.route
reach a runtime in another process. See
.B "Process Isolation"
above.
.PP
The health\-check, circuit\-breaker, retry and TLS families were unreachable
from a configuration until the table above named every flag; a file written
against that build still reads the same way.
.PP
The interpreter that reads this file is not the daemon's, and it is much
smaller. It opens
.BR base ,
.BR package ,
.BR table ,
.B string
and
.B math
\(em there is no
.B os
and no
.BR io .
.RB ` "os.execute(\(dqmkdir -p /tmp/logs\(dq)" `
at the top of a configuration is not a sandbox refusal but
.BR "attempt to index global 'os' (a nil value)" :
the whole file is refused, exit 1, and no runtime is created. Create the
directories a configuration needs from the shell that invokes it.
.B string
and
.B math
are open because a configuration computes names and port numbers, and the
daemon's own configuration file opens the same set, so one file can be read
by either.
.SS Handler Privileges
.B "A Lua handler runs with the daemon's full privileges."
It is not sandboxed and there is no supported way to sandbox it. Read a
handler the way you would read a program you are about to run as the user the
daemon runs as, because that is exactly what it is.
.PP
Which makes the control socket's permission the whole of the boundary. A
handler is attached with
.BR "create \-\-lua" ,
which is an ordinary command on that socket, so whoever may open it may run
code as the daemon's user. The socket is
.B 0600
for a daemon you start yourself, and
.B "0660 socketley:socketley"
for the packaged one \(em membership of that group is therefore equivalent to
being the daemon's user, and should be granted on that understanding. This is
the same arrangement Docker has, and it is worth stating rather than inferring:
nothing else in this daemon stands between a local account and the handler.
.PP
Concretely, a handler can:
.TP
.B run any command
.B io.popen("...")
and
.B os.execute("...")
are both present.
.TP
.B read or write any file that user can
.B io.open()
is present in every mode, including
.BR w ,
which truncates.
.TP
.B load native code
.B package.loadlib()
is present, as are
.BR load (),
.BR loadstring (),
.B dofile()
and
.BR loadfile ().
.TP
.B read the daemon's environment
.B os.getenv()
is present.
.PP
This is deliberate. Handlers legitimately read and write files \(em a logging
pipeline appends to one, a configuration\-driven handler reads its table off
disk \(em so closing
.B io
would break supported use. What it means in practice is that
.B "the right to hand this daemon a \-\-lua script is the right to run code as its user,"
and the thing that controls that right is the permission on the IPC socket, not
anything in the interpreter. See
.B THE IPC SOCKET
under
.BR FILES .
.PP
A handler's interpreter opens
.BR base ,
.BR string ,
.BR table ,
.BR math ,
.BR os ,
.B io
and
.BR package .
Two names are removed from
.BR os ,
and calling one is
.B "attempt to call field '...' (a nil value)"
rather than a refusal with a reason:
.PP
.nf
os.exit    os.setlocale
.fi
.PP
Neither removal is a security boundary \(em nothing here is. The test both
pass is narrower: one call damages the process that every
.I other
runtime in this daemon shares, and does so by accident rather than by intent.
.B os.exit()
ends the daemon from inside a callback, and a handler is very often a
standalone script that grew into one, ending the way standalone scripts end.
.B os.setlocale()
changes the process's
.BR LC_NUMERIC ,
which is what
.B socketley.json_encode
and
.B socketley.json_decode
format and parse numbers with \(em one line in one handler and every other
handler's JSON spells 1.5 as 1,5 and decodes it as 1, silently, for as long as
the daemon lives.
.PP
.BR os.time (),
.BR os.clock (),
.BR os.date ()
and
.BR os.difftime ()
remain: they are reads, and reading the clock or the locale harms nothing.
.PP
Earlier releases also removed
.BR os.execute (),
.BR os.remove (),
.BR os.rename ()
and
.BR os.tmpname ().
Those four are back, because removing them denied no capability \(em
.B io.popen
is a shell and
.B io.open(path, \(dqw\(dq)
truncates \(em while suggesting to anyone reading a handler that the daemon
guarded its files and its shell. It did not. A fence with an open gate beside
it is worse than no fence, because people plan around it.
.PP
Note that
.B io.open
and every write through it block the event loop for as long as the write takes.
.PP
.B require()
resolves against the script's directory and then the default
.BR package.path ,
but
.B package.cpath
is left at its default and no binary module ships with socketley. A
.B require
of a C module \(em
.BR cjson ,
.BR lsqlite3 ,
a
.B luasql
driver \(em therefore fails unless that module is installed where the system Lua
would find it. For JSON, use the built\-in
.B socketley.json_encode
and
.B socketley.json_decode
instead; they need nothing installed.
.SS Available Callbacks
.TP
.B on_start()
Called when runtime starts.
.TP
.B on_stop()
Called when runtime stops.
.TP
.B on_message(msg)
Called when message received (server/client).
When defined on a server, this callback claims message handling: the default broadcast
(in \fBinout\fR and \fBmaster\fR modes) is suppressed. Use \fBself.broadcast(msg)\fR
to relay messages manually.
.TP
.B on_client_message(client_id, msg)
Called when message received with sender identification (server only).
Fires alongside on_message for backward compatibility.
.TP
.B on_http_body(client_id, chunk, remaining)
A piece of a request body, with how many bytes are still owed after it \(em so
.B remaining
reaches 0 exactly once per body. Defining this callback IS the opt\(hyin: a
server whose script has it streams request bodies to it instead of gathering
them, and a server whose script does not gets the behaviour it always had,
including the 413 past
.BR \-\-read\-buffer .
.IP
That is what makes a body larger than memory possible. The bytes are handed
over and erased as they arrive, so
.B req.body
in the
.B on_http_request
that follows is deliberately EMPTY \(em carrying them a second time is the
memory this exists to avoid. Measured on one worker, one connection at a time:
.IP
.nf
start             19,796 kB
after 50 MB #1    37,344 kB
after 50 MB #2    37,344 kB     unchanged
after 50 MB #3    37,344 kB     unchanged
after 200 MB      37,428 kB     +84 kB
after 500 MB      37,444 kB     +16 kB
.fi
.IP
The first upload costs 17.5 MB once, which is the provided\(hybuffer ring being
faulted in, and every one after it costs nothing: half a gigabyte received
inside 37 MB resident.
.IP
Keep per\(hyupload state keyed by
.BR client_id ,
and clear it in
.BR on_disconnect :
a body that is abandoned never reaches
.BR on_http_request ,
so nothing there can be the reset, and
.B on_disconnect
is the only signal it produces. It does fire, with whatever arrived, which is
what a script writing to a temporary file needs in order to remove it.
.IP
.nf
local st = {}
function on_http_body(id, chunk, remaining)
    st[id] = (st[id] or 0) + #chunk
    if remaining == 0 then ... end
end
function on_disconnect(id) st[id] = nil end
.fi
.IP
.B "A body of unknown length."
A client that cannot say how long its body is sends
.B "Transfer-Encoding: chunked"
\(em fetch() with a ReadableStream body, anything piping a process's output,
anything that cannot stat what it is sending \(em and that used to be 501. With
.B on_http_body
defined it is decoded and delivered like any other body, unbounded, because
there is no length to bound it by.
.IP
.B remaining
is
.B nil
for every chunk of such a body, since how much is still owed is genuinely not
knowable, and the end of the framing produces ONE final call with an empty
chunk and
.BR "remaining = 0" .
So "remaining reaches 0 exactly once per body" holds for both forms and a
script written against
.B Content-Length
needs no change. Measured, 62 MB in 1000 chunks: 1708 calls, 1707 of them nil
and one zero; 250 MB after that costs 148 kB more resident memory.
.IP
Chunked and nothing else.
.B "Transfer-Encoding: gzip, chunked"
is framable \(em the chunk sizes do delimit it \(em and its contents are still
compressed, so taking the framing off and handing the bytes on as the body would
hand on gzip and call it the body. That stays 501. A coding that is not chunked
at all leaves the message with no way to say where the body ends and is a 400.
.IP
Trailers are consumed, bounded at 8 KB, and discarded: turning them into
headers after the request has been dispatched is how a trailer becomes request
smuggling. Framing that contradicts itself \(em a chunk not followed by CRLF, a
size that is not hex, a size line past 256 bytes \(em is a 400, because nothing
after it can be trusted to be payload.
.IP
Header names keep the case the client sent them in, so
.B req.headers["Host"]
and not
.BR req.headers["host"] .
.TP
.B on_upstream_writable(conn_id)
An upstream's write queue has come back under
.BR self.watermark() ,
so a producer that stopped because
.B self.upstream_pending(conn_id)
was high can carry on. Its own callback and not
.BR on_writable ,
because an upstream id and a client id are different spaces and one function
taking both would have to be told which it had been given.
.TP
.B on_writable(client_id)
The write queue for this connection has come back down under
.BR self.watermark() ,
so a producer that stopped because
.B self.pending(client_id)
was high can carry on. Edge\(hytriggered: it fires on the crossing, once per
crossing, not once per completed write.
.IP
A client runtime has the same callback without the argument \(em
.B on_writable()
\(em because it has one connection and a number naming it would be a fiction.
Its refusal is named too: a connected
.B \-\-mode out
client whose server has stopped reading used to be told it "is not connected to
its server, or was created \-\-mode in and does not send", three statements
that are all false of it. Measured, 400 chunks of 64 KB into a peer reading
nothing: 129 taken, 271 refused, 8,396,928 bytes standing.
.IP
This is what makes a body larger than the queue bound sendable at all. Without
it a script had the hard bound and nothing leading up to it \(em at 8 MiB the
connection is closed \(em so the only safe strategy was to know the bound in
advance and never approach it. Measured with a client reading in 256 KB bites
with a 5 ms pause, sending 200 chunks of 64 KB (13,107,400 bytes):
.IP
.nf
unpaced   8,454,273 bytes delivered, then the connection closed
paced    13,107,400 bytes delivered, on_writable fired 3 times
.fi
.IP
Pace against
.B self.watermark()
and not a number of your own. It is a call and not a field because
.B \-\-write\-buffer
is editable, and a number copied into the script's table at load would go
stale the moment it moved. The callback fires on the crossing, so a producer
that stops below the watermark never crosses it and is never called back \(em it
would wait for an event its own caution prevented:
.IP
.nf
local function pump(id)
    while remaining > 0 do
        if (self.pending(id) or 0) >= self.watermark() then return end
        if not self.send(id, next_chunk()) then return end
        remaining = remaining \- 1
    end
end

function on_writable(id) pump(id) end
.fi
.IP
The call is made a loop turn after the write that emptied the queue, not on that
write's completion, so a connection that closes in between is simply not called.
.TP
.B on_connect(client_id)
Called when a client connects (server/client). Also called on a sub\-server when
a connection is routed to it. The client_id is opaque: for this runtime's own
clients it is the file descriptor, and for a connection routed here from another
runtime it is a handle above 16777216 that names the connection wherever it
lives. Pass it back to
.BR self.send() ,
.BR self.owner_send() ,
or anything else taking a client_id; do not compare it against a descriptor or
assume anything about its value.
.TP
.B on_disconnect(client_id)
Called when a client disconnects (server/client).
.TP
.B on_send(msg)
Called when message sent.
.TP
.B on_route(method, path, backend_path)
Proxy only, and
.B "\-\-strategy lua"
only. Custom backend selection. Returns backend index (0-based).
Answers only "where does this go"; to accept or refuse a request before any
backend is chosen, see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.IP
The strategy is what consults it. A proxy created without
.B "\-\-strategy lua"
keeps round\-robin (or
.BR random ,
if that was asked for) and never looks the callback up, so a script defining
.B on_route
on such a proxy is loaded, is reported by nothing, and routes exactly as if the
function were not there. That is the usual reason a
.B on_route
that was tested and works appears to do nothing.
.IP
.I path
is the path
.I as the client sent it,
this proxy's own
.BI / name /
prefix included, and
.I backend_path
is the same path with that prefix removed \(em what the backend will actually
receive. A proxy named
.B gw
handed
.B /gw/api/users
sees both:
.nf

function on_route(method, path, backend_path)
    -- path         == "/gw/api/users"
    -- backend_path == "/api/users"
    if backend_path:match("^/api/") then return 0 end
    return 1
end

.fi
Both are offered because both are the truth about a different thing: the filter
chain judges the request the client made, so it is handed
.IR path ,
and a routing decision is usually about the endpoint being addressed, which is
.IR backend_path .
.IP
.I backend_path
exists so that scripts stop computing it. Doing it by hand means stripping
.B "\(dq/\(dq .. self.name .. \(dq/\(dq"
and getting the bare case right, and the bare case is the one nobody gets
right: the gateway's own root arrives as
.B /gw
\(em three characters against a five-character prefix \(em and must become
.BR / ,
where a
.B sub()
past the end yields the empty string and matches no pattern at all.
.IP
The arguments are passed on
.B "\-\-protocol http"
only. A TCP proxy has no request line to read them from and calls
.B on_route
with none, so a body written for HTTP \(em
.RB ` "return hash(path) % n" `
\(em raises on the first request when it is moved to a TCP proxy.
.IP
A handler declaring two parameters is unaffected: Lua discards arguments a
function does not name, so every
.B on_route
written before this existed behaves exactly as it did.
.IP
A call that raises, or that returns anything other than an index inside the
backend list, selects nothing: the proxy reports the error on stderr and falls
back to the selection it would have made without the callback. The request is
forwarded either way. Refusing a request is the filter chain's job, not this
one's.
.TP
.B filters
Proxy only. Not a callback but an array of them: see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.TP
.B routes
Not a callback either, but a table this runtime declares about what it answers,
which the daemon publishes to the proxies in front of it: see
.BR "Declared Routes" .
.TP
.B on_proxy_request(client_id, data) \(-> string | nil
Proxy only. Called for every chunk forwarded from a client to a backend.
Return a modified string to replace the data, or
.B nil
to drop the chunk entirely (the backend never receives it).
.IP
Returning nothing at all forwards the chunk unchanged, and that is not the same
as returning
.BR nil .
A hook written to watch traffic
.RB ( "function on_proxy_request(id, data) log(data) end" )
ends without a return, and reading that as
.B nil
made the most natural three lines in this API discard every byte through the
proxy. The filter chain draws the same distinction \(em nothing returned means
the filter has no opinion and the chain continues.
.IP
Fail\-closed, like the filter chain and for the same reason. A hook that throws
a Lua error, or that returns anything other than a string or
.BR nil ,
refuses the request: the connection to the client and the one to the backend are
both closed, and the reason is logged. The hook is where a script strips a
header or rejects a request, so forwarding the unmodified bytes when it could
not run would admit exactly what it was there to refuse \(em and
.B "return false"
is a rejection, not a value to guess at.
.IP
The refusal closes rather than answering a status: this callback is on the byte
path, is called with body chunks as well as with the request head, and carries
TCP as readily as HTTP. To refuse a request with a status line, use the filter
chain \(em see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.TP
.B on_proxy_response(client_id, data) \(-> string | nil
Proxy only. Called for every chunk forwarded from a backend to a client.
Same semantics as
.BR on_proxy_request :
returning nothing forwards the chunk unchanged, a replacement string replaces
it, nil drops it, and an error or a return of any other type refuses \(em here by closing the client connection, since the
response this hook was to redact must not be delivered unredacted.
.TP
.B on_master_auth(client_id, password) \(-> bool
Server only (master mode). Custom authentication. Return
.B true
to grant the master role. If defined, overrides
.BR \-\-master\-pw ,
so this callback is the entire gate on that server.
Read strictly, and fail\-closed: only the boolean
.B true
grants. Anything else denies \(em
.BR false ,
.BR nil ,
no return at all, and every truthy non\-boolean such as a string, the number 0
or a table. If the callback throws a Lua error the attempt is denied and the
error is logged.
A denial from this callback is counted against the source address exactly as a
wrong
.B \-\-master\-pw
is, so the per-address lockout described there applies here too.
.TP
.B on_auth(client_id) \(-> bool
Server only. Called immediately after a client is accepted, before
.B on_connect
fires. Return
.B true
to admit the client or
.B false
to reject and close the connection. If the callback throws a Lua error, the client is also rejected (fail-closed). When no
.B on_auth
callback is defined, all clients are admitted.
.TP
.B on_websocket(client_id, headers)
Server only. Called immediately after a WebSocket upgrade handshake completes,
after
.B on_connect
has already fired. The
.I headers
argument is a Lua table containing any subset of the following keys from the
HTTP upgrade request:
.BR cookie ,
.BR origin ,
.BR protocol
(Sec-WebSocket-Protocol), and
.BR authorization .
Only keys present in the request are set; absent headers are nil.
This callback never fires for plain TCP clients.
.TP
.B on_http_request(req) \(-> table
Server only. Called when an HTTP request is received (auto-detected from the
first bytes; see
.BR "Multi-Protocol Auto-Detection" ).
The
.I req
argument is a table with the following fields:
.nf
method      string   HTTP method ("GET", "POST", etc.)
path        string   Request path (e.g. "/api/users")
query       string   Query string without leading ? (empty if none)
body        string   Request body (empty for GET)
client_id   number   File descriptor of the client
version     string   HTTP version (e.g. "1.1")
headers     table    Request headers {["Host"]="...", ...}
.fi
.IP
Header names arrive as the client spelled them. Values arrive with
optional whitespace \(em spaces and tabs \(em removed from both ends, so
.B "X\-Token:\ \ abc\ "
reaches the callback as
.BR abc .
The callback is not reached at all by a request whose framing this server
refuses; see
.B "Request Framing"
above for what those are and what they are answered.
.IP
Return a table with the HTTP response:
.nf
{
    status  = 200,                              -- HTTP status code
    headers = {["Content-Type"] = "text/html"}, -- response headers
    body    = "<h1>Hello</h1>"                  -- response body
}
.fi
.IP
If no
.B on_http_request
callback is defined, the server falls back to static file serving
.RB ( \-\-http )
or returns 404. Returning nothing, or anything that is not a table, means the
same thing: the request falls through to the static file or the 404. Only
.B nil
and no return at all are silent about it \(em those are how a handler declines.
Anything else is a script that meant to answer, so the fall-through is named in
the daemon's log as
.BR "[lua <runtime>] on_http_request returned a string; expected a table or nil ..." .
.IP
In the returned table,
.B status
must be a number,
.B body
a string, and
.B headers
a table of string to string. A field the script never set takes its default
(200, an empty body, no headers), but a
.B status
or
.B body
that is present and of the wrong type is a response this server cannot build:
the request is answered
.BR "500 Internal Server Error" ,
and the field and the type it arrived as are written to the daemon's log. A
header whose name or value is not a string costs the client that one header
rather than the whole answer, and is logged the same way. None of these were
reported before, and a
.B body
that was not a string was served as a
.B 200
with nothing in it.
.IP
Supports HTTP/1.1 keep-alive \(em the connection is reused
for subsequent requests unless the client's
.B Connection
header asked to close, which is honoured for a Lua response exactly as it is
for a static file.
.IP
A callback that
.B raises
is not a callback that declined. The request is answered
.BR "500 Internal Server Error" ,
and the error is written to the daemon's log as
.BR "[lua <runtime>] on_http_request error: ..." .
Falling through to the 404 would make a handler that broke indistinguishable,
at the client, from one that had no answer.
.IP
Example:
.nf
function on_http_request(req)
    if req.method == "GET" and req.path == "/health" then
        return {status = 200, body = "ok"}
    end
    return {
        status  = 200,
        headers = {["Content-Type"] = "application/json"},
        body    = '{"method": "' .. req.method .. '", "path": "' .. req.path .. '"}'
    }
end
.fi
.TP
.B on_tick(dt)
Called at a configurable interval while the runtime is running.
.I dt
is the actual elapsed time in milliseconds since the last tick (double).
Requires a top-level
.B tick_ms
global to be set in the Lua script: a whole number of milliseconds, 10 or more,
defaulting to 100 when the global is omitted. A value below 10, or one that is
not a whole number, refuses the script at load with a reason rather than being
rounded into something else \(em 10 is the shortest interval
.B on_tick
can be armed at, and a script that asks for less is asking for something the
daemon will not do.
Example:
.nf
tick_ms = 50
function on_tick(dt)
    self.broadcast("ping")
end
.fi
.TP
.B on_miss(key) \(-> value [, ttl_seconds]
Cache only. Called when
.B GET
finds nothing, so the script can fetch the value from somewhere else; what it
returns is stored and answered as a hit. See
.B "Database Backend"
for the four cache callbacks in full, with their ordering and their failure
behaviour.
.TP
.B on_write(key, value, ttl)
Cache only. Called after a successful
.BR SET / SETEX / SETNX / MSET .
.TP
.B on_delete(key)
Cache only. Called after a
.B DEL
that removed something.
.TP
.B on_expire(key)
Cache only. Called after the TTL sweep removes a key.
.TP
.B on_upstream(conn_id, data)
Server only. Called for each newline-delimited message received from an
upstream connection. Use \-u to configure upstreams.
.TP
.B on_upstream_connect(conn_id)
Server only. Called when an upstream connects (including auto-reconnects).
.TP
.B on_upstream_disconnect(conn_id)
Server only. Called when an upstream disconnects.
.TP
.B on_cluster_join(daemon)
Cluster only. Called when a new remote daemon is discovered. See
.BR "Cluster Event Callbacks" .
.TP
.B on_cluster_leave(daemon)
Cluster only. Called when a remote daemon becomes stale or disappears.
.TP
.B on_group_change(group, members)
Cluster only. Called when the member count of a group changes.
.SS Callback Deadline
Every Lua callback invocation is given a wall\-clock budget. A callback that has
not returned when its budget runs out is stopped with an error, exactly as if
the script had called
.BR error() :
the daemon logs the reason, the callback's usual failure path runs, and the
runtime carries on serving. The default budget is
.B 10000
milliseconds.
.PP
The bound exists because the event loop is a single thread serving every runtime
in the daemon. Before it,
.B "while true do end"
in any callback froze all of them \(em traffic, the metrics endpoint and the IPC
socket \(em with no recovery short of
.BR "kill \-9" .
.PP
What is bounded:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
each invocation of any
.BI on_ *
callback, separately \(em a periodic
.B on_tick
gets a fresh budget on every tick
.IP \(bu 2
each invocation of a
.B socketley.set_timeout
or
.B socketley.set_interval
callback, and of a
.B socketley.subscribe
handler
.IP \(bu 2
the whole
.B filters
chain for one request, as a single budget rather than one per filter
.IP \(bu 2
the script's top level, which runs inside
.BR create .
A top level that never returns refuses the create with a reason instead of
blocking the terminal on a command that never answers
.RE
.PP
This budget is time on the loop and not time on the clock, which the two
callbacks that may wait make visible.
.B on_message
and
.B on_client_message
run on a coroutine, so a handler stopped in
.B self.cache
is not holding the thread and is not spending this: each stretch it actually
runs gets the budget, and how long it may stay stopped is a separate bound of
ten seconds with its own answer. See
.B self.cache
under
.BR "Runtime Methods" .
.PP
A stopped callback is a callback that raised, so wherever a raise already has a
defined answer, the deadline gets that answer.
.B on_auth
denies and says why;
.B on_route
falls back to the usual backend pick; a
.B filters
entry refuses the request with a 500. In
.B on_proxy_request
and
.B on_proxy_response
it is the refusal case and not the "returned nothing" case \(em the connection
closes, and the bytes of a request whose hook never finished deciding are not
forwarded as though the hook had waved them through.
.PP
.B socketley.http
lives inside the same budget: its
.B timeout_ms
is clamped to what is left of the callback's, so a callback that spends its
budget waiting on a host gets an ordinary
.B "ok = false"
result rather than being stopped. A script that needs a long fetch raises the
callback budget to suit. Nothing can interrupt a request mid\-socket \(em the
mechanism is a VM instruction hook and a blocking call executes no instructions.
.PP
.B Setting it.
A script sets its own with a top\-level
.B callback_timeout_ms
global, next to
.BR tick_ms :
.nf

callback_timeout_ms = 30000   \-\- this script does slow work on purpose
callback_timeout_ms = 0       \-\- no bound at all for this script

.fi
An operator moves the default for every script that says nothing with
.B SOCKETLEY_LUA_TIMEOUT_MS
in the daemon's environment; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.PP
.B What it costs.
Not the JIT, which is what this said before it was measured. On this build
LuaJIT compiles traces with a count hook installed; what the hook costs is the
hooked dispatch path, which checks on every VM instruction instead of every ten
thousandth. Measured at 64 bytes on one connection: a callback that merely
answers a message showed no difference above the run\-to\-run noise \(em 42581
and 43130 messages a second with the hook against 42571 and 42644 without \(em
while one doing about 1300 arithmetic operations per message ran at 85% of its
unhooked rate.
.PP
.B What 0 buys.
Setting the bound to
.B 0
installs no hook and also opens LuaJIT's
.B jit
library, which is the only thing that turns the trace compiler on at all:
without it every Lua callback this daemon runs is interpreted. On that same
arithmetic callback the difference is 11722 messages a second bounded against
28085 unbounded, at 85.2 against 30.3 microseconds of worker CPU each.
.PP
A callback that only answers a message gains very little, because there is
almost no loop to compile. On the echo fixture the two arms do not separate in
throughput at all \(em 42850 and 43929 messages a second bounded against 43577
and 44350 unbounded, an arm-to-arm gap smaller than the spread inside either
arm. What does separate is Lua's own share of the worker's CPU, 5.2 and 5.1%
against 3.9 and 4.0%, which is the compiler doing a little work on two
bytecodes. Round it to about 1% end to end, against 2.4x on a callback that
computes.
.PP
A streaming producer sits between those two and had never been measured, which
matters because it is the shape that runs per byte rather than per message. A
handler generating 40,000 CSV rows into
.B self.send_chunk
gains 25 to 48 per cent, not 2.4x \(em and the profile says why: with the hook
installed the interpreter is 92.7 per cent of the worker's core, but what is
inside that is
.BR lj_str_new ,
.BR lj_str_resize ,
.B gc_sweepstr
and the allocator, about 35 per cent between them. A compiled trace allocates
and interns every string it builds exactly as the interpreter does, so the
compiler removes the dispatch and leaves the string machinery standing.
.PP
Which makes how the script builds its strings the larger lever, and the two
multiply rather than add. The same handler with
.B string.format
in place of a chain of
.B ..
concatenations, at 8 concurrent requests and then 64:
.IP
.nf
                        bounded      unbounded
\&.. concatenation        97.8/s      130.6/s   (+34%)
string.format           93.8/s      160.0/s   (+71%)
\&.. at 64 concurrent      68.0/s      100.8/s   (+48%)
string.format at 64     71.5/s      142.7/s  (+100%)
.fi
.IP
Both arms put byte\-identical responses on the wire. Note the second column is
what changes:
.B string.format
is worth nothing in the interpreter \(em 4 per cent behind the concatenation,
inside the spread \(em because there it is one C call per row either way. It
pays only once the compiler is on, and then it pays twice: one string per row
instead of a chain of intermediates, and an integer formatter instead of the
double one, which was 15.6 per cent of the core on its own. End to end, from
the default with concatenation to the compiler with
.BR string.format ,
that is 64 per cent more throughput at 8 streams and 110 per cent at 64, for
about half the CPU per request.
.PP
A C server building the same rows with
.BR snprintf (3)
still serves 2.4x what the best of those does, so this is a decision about how
much of a stream's work belongs in Lua at all \(em not a setting that closes
the gap.
.PP
.B What decides the size of the gain
is how much of the work is at the Lua level, not how many bytes pass through.
Measured on a proxy, which is a second runtime type with a per\(hybyte hook, both
handlers forwarding the bytes untouched so only the work differs:
.IP
.nf
                              bounded   unbounded   CPU per request
one C library call per chunk    120.9      121.9     8223 -> 8155 us
a Lua loop over every row       417.1     1452.0     2378 ->  669 us
.fi
.IP
The first is a
.B gsub
over each chunk and gains one per cent: the call is C either way and there is
almost no Lua around it to compile. The second walks the rows in a Lua
.B while
loop and gains 248 per cent at 72 per cent less CPU. Every runtime type reads the
same setting from the same place, so the five shapes measured so far order
themselves by that one question \(em about one per cent for a C call per byte or
a callback that only answers, 34 to 48 per cent for building strings (where the
allocator and the intern table are the cost and the compiler cannot remove them),
2.4x for arithmetic, and 248 per cent for Lua control flow over many items.
.PP
.B How the reply is assembled decides both,
and the number of pieces decides whether it matters at all. Four idioms building
one reply out of N parts, on a server, one binary and one C client, interleaved,
each pair of rows below the same script with the compiler closed and open:
.IP
.nf
  N = 24, compiler closed       exchanges/s   spread
    s = s .. piece                 160,495      22%
    s = s .. string.format(..)     153,658      24%
    t[i] = piece, concat once      159,721      23%
    t[i] = format, concat once     166,023      15%
.fi
.IP
Nothing there is a finding: every spread is larger than every difference. A short
reply is assembled the way that reads best, and reaching for
.B string.format
because it is faster is reaching for something that is not there.
.IP
.nf
  N = 1000                      exchanges/s   us cpu/req   spread
    s = s .. piece                   1,251         801      6%
                  compiler open      1,426         703      6%
    s = s .. string.format(..)         923       1,087      8%
                  compiler open        843       1,192      5%
    t[i] = piece, concat once        7,618         131      6%
                  compiler open     13,361          75      2%
    t[i] = format, concat once       8,656         115      7%
                  compiler open     17,675          57      5%
.fi
.IP
At a thousand pieces the best shape is 14.1 times the worst at a fourteenth of
the CPU per request. Joining once is most of it \(em 6.1x on its own \(em and the
formatter is worth a further 14 per cent behind it, against being 26 per cent
.I slower
when it is used to accumulate instead. The same call, opposite signs, decided by
the shape around it.
.IP
What it does to the compiler is the part worth keeping. Opening it is worth 14
per cent on the accumulating shape, nothing outside the spread on the
accumulating one that formats, and 75 and 104 per cent on the two that join
once. That is the rule above seen from the other side: accumulating moves the
work out of Lua and into the allocator, where there is nothing left for the
compiler to reach. A script that is slow because of its idiom does not become
fast because of the setting.
.PP
.B What 0 gives up.
A compiled trace does not call an instruction hook back, so with the compiler
on a runaway callback cannot be interrupted at all. Measured:
.B "while true do end"
against a 300 millisecond deadline stopped in 300.2 milliseconds with the
compiler off and never with it on, and a hard ceiling inside the hook did not
help either, because the hook is not reached from inside a trace. That is why
the two are one setting rather than two knobs that can disagree.
.PP
The blast radius is one runtime rather than the daemon. Measured with a server
whose callback never returns: its own worker process sat at 83% of a core while
a second server on the same daemon went on echoing and the daemon went on
answering
.BR ps .
A runaway in the script's top level is still refused outright whatever
.B callback_timeout_ms
says, because the top level runs under the default before the script's own
declaration has been read.
.PP
.B What it does not bound.
A callback stopped at its deadline has still held the loop for that long, so a
CLI command issued during the stall can hit its own five second receive timeout
and report the daemon unreachable; it succeeds on the retry. A runaway
.B on_tick
is stopped on every tick rather than once. And
.BR getaddrinfo (3),
inside
.BR socketley.http ,
executes no VM instructions and is not interrupted.
.SS Proxy Filter Chain
Proxy only, HTTP mode only.
.B filters
is a global array of functions run in order on each request \fIbefore\fR a
backend is selected. Each filter either says nothing \(em carry on to the next
\(em or returns the answer, which ends the request there.
.PP
The ordering is the point. A request a filter refuses never reaches backend
selection, so no endpoint is resolved, no circuit breaker's half-open probe is
claimed, no pooled socket is spent and no connection is opened. This is where
authentication, rate limiting and header rewriting belong;
.B on_route
runs after a backend has already been chosen and answers a different question.
.PP
The chain is independent of
.BR \-\-strategy .
It runs whatever picks the backend afterwards, and on a proxy with no
.B on_route
at all. A proxy whose script declares no
.B filters
array pays nothing: the chain is guarded by the same callback mask as every
other hook.
.PP
Each filter is called with one argument:
.nf
method      string   HTTP method ("GET", "POST", ...)
path        string   Request path as the client sent it, this proxy's
                     prefix included \(em the same path on_route sees
version     string   HTTP version (e.g. "HTTP/1.1")
client_id   number   File descriptor of the client; pass it to
                     self.peer_ip() to identify the peer
headers     table    Request headers. Names are lowercased, values
                     are trimmed, and a field sent more than once is
                     joined with ", ".
.fi
.PP
And returns one of three things:
.TP
.B nil
(or nothing) \(em this filter has no opinion; the next one runs.
.TP
.BI "{ status = " N ", body = " string ", headers = " table " }"
The answer. The chain stops, the request is never forwarded, and this response
goes to the client.
.I status
must be 200\-599.
.I body
and
.I headers
are optional. The connection is closed afterwards, as it is for every other
response the proxy writes itself.
.TP
.BI "{ set_headers = " table " }"
Carry on, with these headers set on the request that goes to the backend. A
header set here replaces one the client sent under the same name rather than
joining it, and is emitted with a lowercased name.
.TP
.BI "{ remove_headers = { " name ", " name ", ... } }"
Carry on, with these headers taken off the request before it goes to the
backend \(em every occurrence of each name, so a field the client sent twice is
removed entirely. For a header the backend must never see whatever the client
sent: an internal trust header a caller forged, a cookie with no business past
the edge.
.I set_headers
and
.I remove_headers
may appear in the same table. A name that is both set and removed takes the
later of the two statements, exactly as two
.I set_headers
for one name resolve.
.PP
A filter that returns anything else, or that raises an error, refuses the
request with 500. A filter that did not say "carry on" is never treated as
though it had. An entry in
.B filters
that is not a function refuses the whole script at load time, for the same
reason: a silently dropped filter looks exactly like one that admits everybody.
.PP
Names a filter may not set \fIor remove\fR are dropped, with a line on stderr:
.B Content\-Length
and
.B Transfer\-Encoding
on either side, because the proxy forwards exactly the body the parsed request
declared and a filter that could change the length would make the proxy and the
backend disagree about where the request ends;
.B Host
on the request, because the proxy rewrites it to the endpoint it selected; and
the hop-by-hop set
.RB ( Connection ", " Keep\-Alive ", " TE ", " Trailer ", " Upgrade ", " Proxy\-* ).
So is any name that is not a bare token and any value carrying CR or LF \(em a
filter is frequently echoing something the client supplied.
.PP
Filters run on the event loop thread. Whatever a filter does, every request on
the proxy waits for: no blocking, no synchronous I/O, no long loops.
.PP
Example:
.nf
filters = {
    function(req)
        if not req.headers["authorization"] then
            return {
                status  = 401,
                body    = "authentication required\\n",
                headers = {["WWW-Authenticate"] = "Bearer"},
            }
        end
        return {set_headers = {["x-authenticated-user"] = "alice"}}
    end,
}
.fi
.IP
See
.B examples/proxy/filter\-chain.lua
for authentication, per-IP rate limiting and header rewriting as three filters
in one chain.
.SS Declared Routes
.B routes
is a global array in which a runtime states what it will answer: a path, and
the methods allowed on it. It is declared in the \fIhandler's\fR script and
published to the proxies that forward to that runtime, which then refuse those
methods at the edge \(em before any endpoint is resolved, any socket is taken
from the pool and any connection is opened.
.PP
The declaration lives with the handler on purpose. Configured on the proxy, a
method list is a second copy of something the handler already states in its own
code, in another runtime and soon in another process, and two copies drift: the
handler grows
.BR PATCH ,
the gateway goes on refusing it, and the 405 is explicable from neither side
alone. Nothing is configured on the proxy; editing the handler's script and
running
.B socketley reload\-lua
changes what the gateway enforces.
.PP
.nf
routes = {
    { path = "/endpoint/login", methods = {"POST", "GET"} },
    { path = "/static/*",       methods = {"GET"} },
}
.fi
.PP
.B path
is the path as the \fIhandler\fR sees it \(em the proxy's own
.BI / name /
prefix already stripped \(em and a query string is not part of it. A trailing
.B *
makes it a literal prefix; that is the whole of the pattern language. Exact
matches win over prefixes, and the longest prefix wins among prefixes.
.B methods
is a list of method names; a bare string is accepted for a single one. The
declaration is uppercased when the script loads, so
.B "methods = {\(dqpost\(dq}"
declares POST. The method a client sends is compared as sent \(em RFC 9110 9.1
makes the method token case-sensitive, and a request for
.B post
is one the handler would refuse too.
.PP
What the proxy does with it:
.TP
A declared method
is forwarded, as always.
.TP
An undeclared method on a declared path
is answered
.B 405 Method Not Allowed
with an
.B Allow
header listing what would have worked. No backend is involved.
.TP
.B HEAD
is allowed wherever
.B GET
is (RFC 9110 9.3.2) \(em a handler that serves GET serves HEAD whether or not it
listed it.
.TP
.B OPTIONS
is never refused. A plain OPTIONS \(em "what may I do here", RFC 9110 9.3.7 \(em
is answered
.B 204
with
.B Allow
out of the declaration. A CORS \fIpreflight\fR (an OPTIONS carrying
.B Origin
and
.BR Access\-Control\-Request\-Method )
is always forwarded to the handler instead: the declaration says nothing about
origins, and an answer without
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin
breaks the preflight exactly as a 405 would \(em the real request is never sent
and the browser reports a CORS error. A handler that declares
.B OPTIONS
itself always gets it.
.TP
A path nobody declared
is forwarded, whatever the method. A declaration speaks about the paths it names
and says nothing about the others; it is not a whitelist, and declaring one
route does not hide the rest.
.TP
A backend given as \fIhost:port\fR
has no declaration and never will \(em the published snapshot is keyed by
runtime name and by group. Such a backend is forwarded to as before.
.PP
For a proxy with several backends, or an
.BI @ group
backend, one rule covers both halves of the fold: nothing may be refused that
\fIeither\fR possible target might have served. A path only some of them declare
is dropped \(em a runtime that never mentioned it may well serve it. A path all
of them declare keeps the \fIunion\fR of their methods, since the proxy does not
know which target a request will reach. One backend or group member that
declares nothing therefore leaves that proxy enforcing nothing at all, silently
and on purpose: it has said nothing about what it answers, so nothing can be
refused on its account.
.PP
.B "Edge rejection is not input validation."
The proxy can confirm the method is one the handler declared. It cannot confirm
the body is a well-formed login, and it is not the only way in: a socketley
server listens on its own port, and a request that arrives there passed no edge
at all. A handler that stops checking its input because "the gateway checks" is
safe only for as long as every request comes through the gateway, which is a
property of the deployment and not of the code. Declare routes to save the
round trip and to say what you mean; go on validating.
.PP
.B socketley stats \fIproxy\fR
reports
.B declared_routes
\(em how many routes that proxy is actually enforcing. It is the first thing to
look at when a method that should be refused is not: a backend that declares
nothing, a backend named by address, a script that failed to load and a proxy
whose handler had not started yet all look identical from the client side, and
all of them read 0 here.
.PP
A malformed entry refuses the script at load time, with the reason on stderr,
rather than being dropped: a declaration that is not the one in the file is
invisible from both sides. A
.B routes
table with no entry at index 1 is refused too \(em it is an array, and
.B routes = {["/a"] = {"GET"}}
would otherwise load and declare nothing.
.PP
Neither end of this has to be in the daemon. A proxy in a worker process of its
own enforces declarations the same way: the table reaches it in the endpoint
frame that already carries the addresses. And a
.I handler
in a worker declares them the same way, which is less obvious \(em the
declaration is produced by running the script, and only the worker runs it, so
it travels back to the daemon in a
.B routes
frame on the control socket and the placeholder publishes the worker's copy.
Where either runtime runs makes no difference to which methods are refused.
.IP
See
.B examples/proxy/declared\-routes.lua
for a handler that declares an AJAX endpoint and the gateway in front of it.
.SS Runtime Methods (in Lua)
.nf
-- Every type
self.name      -- Runtime name (read-only)
self.port      -- Port number (read-only)
self.state     -- Current state (read-only)
self.type      -- Runtime type (read-only)
self.connections()         -- Current connection count (all four types).
                              With --shards, the whole runtime's count summed
                              across its worker processes, not the shard's own

self.protocol  -- server/client: "tcp" or "udp"; proxy: "tcp" or "http"
               -- (not set on a cache)
self.send(msg)             -- Send message (client)
self.send(client_id, msg)  -- Send to specific client (server)
self.broadcast(msg)        -- Broadcast to all clients (server).  Answers two
                           -- numbers: how many took it, and how many were
                           -- dropped because their queue was full.  See below.
self.pending(client_id)    -- Queued for that client and not yet on the wire,
                           -- in bytes (server); nil if the id is not one of
                           -- this runtime's connections.  See below.
self.respond_stream(id, status, headers)
                           -- Begin a response this script will write itself,
                           -- chunk by chunk (server).  See below.
self.send_chunk(id, data)  -- One chunk of it; false means it was not taken
self.end_response(id)      -- The last chunk has gone; finish the response
self.watermark()           -- The byte count on_writable fires on the way down
                           -- through (server and client).  Pace against this,
                           -- not a literal.  A call and not a field, because
                           -- --write-buffer is editable.  See on_writable.
on_http_body(id, chunk, n) -- A request body streamed, rather than gathered
                           -- whole (server).  Defining it is the opt-in.
self.pending()             -- The client's own form, without an id: a client
                           -- has one connection.  Bytes queued for its server
                           -- and not yet on the wire.

-- ─── Sending faster than the socket ───
-- A write is queued, not performed, and the queue has a bound.  Reach it and
-- the runtime closes the connection -- which used to be the only way a script
-- found out: the message that filled the queue was dropped and answered true,
-- and the one after it was refused with "there is no open connection", the
-- sentence a peer hanging up gets.
--
-- self.send() now answers false for a message that was not taken, and names
-- the backlog when that is the reason, so a producer can tell "slow down" from
-- "it is gone".  self.pending() is the number to watch BEFORE sending, which
-- is the difference between pacing and a post-mortem:
--
--     if (self.pending(id) or 0) >= self.watermark() then return end
--
-- and on_writable(id) is how a producer that stopped there is started again,
-- which is what makes a body past the bound sendable rather than merely
-- survivable.  See on_writable in CALLBACKS.
--
-- self.broadcast() has the same bound at its own door, and used to report
-- nothing at all: a broadcast to 3000 clients that dropped a third of them was
-- indistinguishable from one that reached every single one.  It answers two
-- numbers now --
--
--     local taken, behind = self.broadcast(msg)
--
-- counting THIS shard's connections, which is the scope a script can act on; a
-- held message counts as taken, because an unclassified connection's hold is a
-- deferred take and not a drop.  And a client dropped by the broadcast gets
-- the backlog sentence from a later self.send() rather than the peer's, which
-- it did not before -- one flag, both entrances.
--
-- Measured with a client that connects and never reads, against 400 chunks of
-- 64 KB: 129 taken, 271 refused, and the queue standing at its bound.

-- ─── A response written rather than returned ───
-- on_http_request has to RETURN its body, so a generated answer had to fit in
-- memory.  A FILE of any size was already served by the splice path and a Range
-- of it by the 206 path; a generated one of any size was not expressible at all
-- -- transcoding, an export assembled from a cache, a CSV of ten million rows.
--
--     self.respond_stream(id, 200, {["Content-Type"] = "text/csv"})
--     for row in rows() do
--         if (self.pending(id) or 0) >= self.watermark() then return end   -- wait
--         self.send_chunk(id, row)
--     end
--     self.end_response(id)
--
-- Paced through self.pending()/on_writable like any other producer, which is
-- what makes it a stream rather than a slower way to run out of memory.
-- Measured: 200 MB of generated rows through an 8 MiB queue, delivered in
-- 51,200 chunks with on_writable resuming it 67 times, for 6 MB of resident
-- memory.
--
-- The handler returns nothing after starting one: the response is already on
-- its way, and the static-file and 404 fallbacks are skipped for this request.
-- A handler that only tried to send a chunk has NOT answered, so its ordinary
-- return still applies.
--
-- Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding and Connection are dropped from the header
-- table if given: a Content-Length beside a chunked body is a message carrying
-- two answers to its own length, which this server refuses with a 400 on the
-- way in.  Measured with it passed through: the client read 119 bytes where 5
-- were sent, having believed the wrong header.
--
-- send_chunk on a HEAD request answers false and names it, rather than dropping
-- the bytes: a HEAD asked for the headers and nothing else, and a script that
-- thinks it is sending a body to one has a bug this refusal is the only report
-- of.  An empty chunk is accepted and not sent, because a zero-length chunk is
-- how the terminator is spelled on the wire -- passing one through as payload
-- would end the response while the script was still writing it.

.fi
.PP
.nf
-- A question whose answer is somewhere else (server with --cache)
self.cache(command)        -- Run one command against the LINKED cache and
                           -- answer with what it said.  The handler STOPS here
                           -- and goes on when the reply arrives; the daemon
                           -- keeps serving meanwhile.  nil plus a reason when
                           -- the answer is not coming.

-- ─── A handler that waits ───
-- Every callback used to run to the end in one go, so a script could answer
-- only out of what it already held.  A server linked to a cache could not read
-- that cache at all, and the one call that reaches outside the process --
-- socketley.http -- does it by blocking the event loop for every runtime in the
-- daemon, which is why its own entry says to use it in on_start and nowhere
-- else.
--
--     function on_client_message(id, msg)
--         local v, err = self.cache("get " .. msg)
--         self.send(id, v or ("miss: " .. err))
--     end
--
-- The handler runs on a coroutine.  At the call it suspends, the request goes
-- to the cache, and the event loop carries on with every other connection and
-- every other runtime; when the reply lands the handler picks up at the same
-- line with the answer as that call's return value.  Nothing else in the script
-- changes, and a handler that never calls it is not affected: a callback that
-- runs straight through costs the same as it did, measured at 0.158 us against
-- 0.161 for the plain call it replaced, because the coroutines are pooled.
--
-- Where it may be called from: on_message and on_client_message.  Anywhere
-- else -- a timer, on_http_request, a filter -- answers nil and says so,
-- because those call sites still expect an answer on the spot.
--
-- Three things are guaranteed, and each exists because the shape without it
-- was reachable:
--
--   * It always returns.  A cache that accepts the request and never answers
--     leaves the handler stopped for ever otherwise -- measured against a
--     SIGSTOPped cache worker, whose request is parked against a ticket nothing
--     completes.  After ten seconds the call answers nil, "timeout".
--
--   * The connection is still there.  While the handler waits, the connection
--     it is answering is held open exactly as a client's own `cache get` is:
--     a promised reply, which the close path defers on.  Without it every
--     `echo | nc` fails, because the peer half-closes the moment it has sent
--     its line and the answer does not exist yet.
--
--   * It cannot pile up.  4096 stopped callbacks per runtime, and a server that
--     is already owed as many answers as one connection may have refuses the
--     call with a reason rather than stopping the handler.
--
-- The command is the cache's own text protocol, so the answer is that
-- protocol's: `get` on a key that is not there answers the string "nil" rather
-- than a Lua nil, which is the same answer a client reading the port gets.
.fi
.PP
.nf
-- Upstream actions (server with -u)
self.upstream_send(conn_id, msg) -- Send to a specific upstream; false means
                                 -- the bytes are not going.  See below.
self.upstream_broadcast(msg)     -- Send to all connected upstreams.  Answers how
                                 -- many took it and how many were dropped
self.upstream_pending(conn_id)   -- Bytes queued for that upstream and not yet on
                                 -- the wire; nil if the id is not one of this
                                 -- runtime's upstreams
self.upstreams()                 -- Table of connected upstream conn_ids

-- ─── The same bound, pointed outwards ───
-- An upstream's write queue fills exactly as a client's does, and these used to
-- report none of it.  upstream_send() tested only whether the upstream was
-- connected; a queue that reached the bound sets `closing` and leaves
-- `connected` alone, so every later send answered TRUE for ever while nothing
-- left the process.  Measured, 400 sends of 64 KB at an upstream that accepted
-- the connection and then read nothing:
--
--     before   sent=400  failed=0    with 8,396,928 bytes standing in the queue
--     after    sent=129  failed=271  the same 129/271 the client-facing side
--                                    reports, because it is the same bound
--
-- The refusal names the backlog apart from a missing upstream, because "is not
-- connected" is false of a connected upstream whose queue this runtime filled --
-- and the two want opposite reactions.  Pace with self.upstream_pending() and
-- not self.pending(): that one resolves a CLIENT id, and an upstream is in
-- another map entirely.
--
-- on_upstream_writable(conn_id) is the other half, and without it the number
-- above is only a post-mortem: a producer could ask how deep the queue was and
-- never be told when there was room.  The same 400 sends of 64 KB, at an
-- upstream that reads:
--
--     unpaced   sent=129  failed=271   and the upstream closed
--     paced     sent=400  refused=0    resumed 6 times
--
-- Same messages, same bound, same upstream.  129 of 400 is what a producer that
-- does not look gets, and it is not a limit on how much can be sent.

-- Cache: mode
-- A call and not a field, because --mode is editable and a field written when
-- this table was built would go stale.  "readonly", "readwrite" or "admin" --
-- the same three names --mode, `show` and the wire protocol use.
self.cache_mode()   -- Which mode this cache is in

-- Cache: strings
self.get(key)       -- Get string value
self.set(key, val)  -- Set string value; false in readonly mode
self.del(key)       -- Delete key (any type); false in readonly mode
self.exists(key)    -- Is the key there?  See below.

-- Cache: lists
self.lpush(key, val)        -- Push to front; false in readonly mode
self.rpush(key, val)        -- Push to back; false in readonly mode
self.lpop(key)              -- Pop from front; "" in readonly mode
self.rpop(key)              -- Pop from back; "" in readonly mode
self.llen(key)              -- List length

-- Cache: sets
self.sadd(key, member)      -- 1 added, 0 already a member,
                            -- nil refused (readonly mode)
self.srem(key, member)      -- Remove member; false in readonly mode
self.sismember(key, member) -- Check membership
self.scard(key)             -- Set cardinality

-- Cache: hashes
self.hset(key, field, val)  -- Set hash field; false in readonly mode
self.hget(key, field)       -- Get hash field
self.hdel(key, field)       -- Delete hash field; false in readonly mode
self.hexists(key, field)    -- Is the field there?  See below.
self.hlen(key)              -- Hash field count

-- Cache: TTL
self.expire(key, seconds)   -- Set expiry; false in readonly mode
self.ttl(key)               -- Get remaining TTL
self.persist(key)           -- Remove expiry; false in readonly mode

-- Cache: pub/sub
self.publish(channel, msg)  -- Publish to channel

-- Server: connection control
-- Every mutator below answers `true`, or `false, "reason"`.  They returned
-- nothing at all before, so an id that named no connection was a silent no-op.
self.disconnect(client_id)         -- Terminate a connection from Lua
self.peer_ip(client_id)            -- Get client IP (IPv4 or IPv6 string)
                                   -- also on a proxy, same meaning
self.ws_headers(client_id)         -- Get WS upgrade headers table (nil for TCP)

-- Server: client enumeration and multicast
self.clients()                     -- Return table of all connected client_ids
                                   -- also on a proxy, same meaning
self.multicast(ids, msg)           -- Send msg to a subset of clients (ids = array of
                                   -- client_ids).  Returns how many were reached.
                                   -- An entry that is not a whole number refuses the
                                   -- whole call rather than being dropped from it.

-- Server: named groups
self.join_group(id, group)         -- Add client to a named group
self.leave_group(id, group)        -- Remove client from a group
self.group_send(group, msg)        -- Send msg to all clients in a group.  Returns how
                                   -- many were reached; 0 is a group with nobody in it.

-- Server: zero-copy pipe pairing (bypasses Lua callbacks)
self.pipe(id_a, id_b)             -- Pair two clients: data forwarded directly between them
self.unpipe(id)                    -- Remove pipe pairing for a client
socketley.pipe(src, fd_a, src, fd_b)   -- Pair two connections of one server
socketley.pipe(src, fd, dst)           -- Route a connection to another server.
                                   -- Despite the name it hands no descriptor over:
                                   -- src keeps the socket and relays the bytes

-- Server: per-connection metadata
self.set_data(id, key, val)        -- Set string metadata on a connection (nil val deletes key)
self.get_data(id, key)             -- Get metadata string or nil

-- Server: client routing
self.route(client_id, target)      -- Forward client to sub-server, in this or
                                   -- any other process. This server keeps the
                                   -- socket and relays; the target answers.
self.unroute(client_id)            -- Stop forwarding
self.get_route(client_id)          -- Get current route (string or nil)

-- Server: owner-targeted sending (sub-server only)
self.owner_send(client_id, msg)    -- Send to a connection routed here, through
                                   -- the runtime that owns it. Returns false if
                                   -- that connection has gone.
self.owner_broadcast(msg)          -- Broadcast to all owner's clients

-- Proxy
self.clients()                     -- Table of connected client_ids
self.peer_ip(client_id)            -- Client IP (IPv4 or IPv6 string)
self.close_client(client_id)       -- Close one client connection
self.backends()                    -- Array of backend addresses, in the
                                   -- order on_route indexes them (1-based
                                   -- in Lua; on_route returns 0-based)
self.backend_health()              -- Circuit-breaker state per backend,
                                   -- same 1-based order:
                                   -- "closed" | "open" | "half_open"
self.stats()                       -- {connections, bytes_in, bytes_out,
                                   --  total_connections, total_messages,
                                   --  backend_count}
.fi
.PP
.B "Why exists and hexists are there."
.B self.get
answers with the value, and an absent key and a key holding an empty string
both come back as the empty string.
A script cannot tell those apart, and this page's own example leans on it \(em
.I examples/cache/cache\-integration\-handler.lua
reads
.B "self.get(\(dqconfig:timeout\(dq) == \(dq\(dq"
to mean "not set yet".
.PP
Making
.B get
answer
.B nil
for an absent key would settle it and would break that reading wherever it has
been written, and an empty string is a value a cache is entitled to hold. So the
missing question was added instead, under the name the wire protocol has always
used for it.
.nf
.fi
.PP
.B "The proxy table is smaller than the server's on purpose."
A proxy relays between a client and a backend and speaks for neither, so there
is no
.BR broadcast() ,
no
.B send()
and no per\-connection metadata: it has no message of its own to put on the
wire. To change what crosses it, use
.B on_proxy_request
and
.BR on_proxy_response ,
and to refuse a request, the filter chain.
.PP
.B self.backends()
is indexed from 1, like every Lua array, while
.B on_route
returns an index from 0 \(em so the address
.B on_route
selects with
.B n
is
.BR "self.backends()[n + 1]" .
.PP
.B self.owner_send()
takes only a client_id this runtime was actually given \(em one that arrived
through
.BR self.route() ,
reported by
.BR on_connect() .
It answers
.B false
for anything else, including a client_id whose connection has since closed. It
used to take any number and write it into the owner's connection table as a
descriptor index, so a script holding an id past its connection's life wrote
into whichever client had inherited that descriptor.
.SS Global Functions
.TP
.B socketley.log(msg)
Write one line to the daemon's log at
.B INFO
level, prefixed with the name of the runtime that wrote it:
.BR "[12:04:31] [INFO] [lua my\-server] ..." .
It goes through the daemon's logger, so
.B log_level
in the configuration file turns it down like anything else; at the default
level nothing that used to appear stops appearing. Every Lua diagnostic carries
the same
.B [lua <runtime>]
prefix, which is what separates two scripts sharing one stderr \(em and two
runtimes commonly share one script file, so the file name in an error is not an
identity.
.TP
.B socketley.set_timeout(ms, fn) \(-> timer_id
Fire
.I fn
once after
.I ms
milliseconds using an io_uring timeout SQE.
Returns an integer timer ID that can be passed to
.BR socketley.clear_timeout() .
Shares the event loop with the runtime \(em no extra threads.
.TP
.B socketley.set_interval(ms, fn) \(-> timer_id
Fire
.I fn
every
.I ms
milliseconds. Returns an integer timer ID that can be passed to
.BR socketley.clear_interval() .
.IP
Both kinds are cancelled when the runtime stops, and are
.B not
restored if it is started again. A stopped runtime executes no script, so a
timer armed by one does not survive it \(em including a timer armed inside
.BR on_stop ,
which is severed with the rest. The registrations are made at the script's top
level, which runs once, when the runtime's worker is forked \(em so a
.B start
after a
.B stop
forks a new worker, runs the top level again, and arms them again. What is not
replayed is anything the previous worker held: a timer is not restored, it is
re-armed by a script that is being run afresh.
.IP
Both return
.B 0
and arm nothing when
.I ms
is not greater than zero, when the runtime has no event loop, or when the
runtime has been stopped \(em the last of those covers the script that calls
.B socketley.stop
on its own runtime and carries on, since
.B stop
returns to it and everything after that line runs on a runtime that is already
down. A
.B start
lifts it, before
.BR on_start ,
so a restarted runtime arms exactly as it did the first time.
.B socketley.subscribe
answers 0 in the same situation and for the same reason. Real ids
start at 1, so
.B 0
is unambiguous; the reason is written to the daemon's log, because a timer that
will never fire is otherwise silent.
.TP
.B socketley.clear_timeout(timer_id)
Cancel a pending timeout. The timer will not fire. Safe to call with
an already-expired or invalid ID (no-op).
.TP
.B socketley.clear_interval(timer_id)
Cancel a repeating interval. The timer stops after the current cycle
completes. Safe to call with an already-expired or invalid ID (no-op).
.IP
Both withdraw the timer rather than merely muting it: the io_uring timeout is
removed, the timer's storage is released, and the reference to the callback
goes with it \(em so the closure and everything it captured become collectable
at the call rather than when the timer would have expired. Cancelling a
one-hour interval used to hold all of that for the hour.
.IP
Both are safe to call from inside the timer's own callback, which is how a
one-shot repeating timer is written.
.TP
.B socketley.json_encode(value) \(-> string | false, why
Encode a Lua value (table, string, number, boolean, nil) as a JSON string.
Tables with consecutive integer keys starting at 1 are encoded as JSON
arrays; all others as objects.
.IP
Object keys are written in sorted order, so encoding equal data twice gives the
same string. A Lua table has no order of its own \(em one filled
.IR alpha ", " beta ", " gamma ", " delta
iterates
.IR beta ", " gamma ", " delta ", " alpha
\(em so without sorting the output was not a function of the value, and two
tables holding the same data could encode differently. Anything that compares
encoded documents depends on this: an ETag, a cache key, a signature, a
deduplication hash, or a diff of two logged payloads.
.IP
Refused, with
.BR false " and a reason, when the value cannot be written as a document:"
a table that contains itself (directly or through any chain of fields), or one
nested deeper than 128 levels. A table reachable twice by different paths is
not a cycle and encodes normally.
.IP
A string is truthy and
.B false
is not, so
.B "local s = socketley.json_encode(t)"
reads unchanged and
.B "if not s then"
is how the refusal is tested.
.TP
.B socketley.json_decode(str) \(-> value | false, why
Decode a JSON string into a Lua value. Objects become tables with string
keys, arrays become tables with integer keys (1-based). Null becomes nil.
.IP
Refused, with
.BR false " and a reason, for a document nested deeper than 128 levels, for"
bytes that are not a complete JSON document, and for trailing data after the
document ends.
.IP
The refusal on malformed input is what makes the return value trustworthy. The
decoder used to answer with whatever it had understood so far, so
.B [1,2
\(em a request body cut short by a dropped connection \(em decoded to an
ordinary two-element table,
.B {"a":}
to an empty one, and
.BR nul ", " garbage
and an empty string all to nil, which was also the answer for a document that
really was
.BR null .
A handler could not tell a truncated body from a whole one and carried on with
the fields that happened to arrive.
.IP
This limit is not a formatting preference. This call is written to be pointed
at data from the network \(em a request body, a message payload \(em so the
nesting depth is chosen by whoever sent it, and the decoder recurses to follow
it. Without the limit a few hundred kilobytes of
.B [[[[
took the whole daemon down, and no callback deadline covers it: those measure
Lua execution, and this recursion is native.
.TP
.B socketley.subscribe(cache_name, channel, fn) \(-> subscription_id
Register
.I fn(channel, message)
to be called whenever the named cache runtime publishes to
.IR channel .
The function is called on the subscribing runtime's event loop turn (not from the cache itself).
Multiple subscriptions to the same channel are all fired in registration order.
.IP
Returns an integer id for
.BR socketley.unsubscribe() .
Ids start at 1 and are never reused, so
.B 0
is a refusal: it means the runtime has been stopped and this registration would
have outlived the stop that ended the others. See
.B socketley.set_interval
for the whole of it \(em the two refuse together and a
.B start
lifts both.
.IP
Only a
.B running
runtime is delivered to. Every registration this one holds is ended when it
stops, and one that has never been started receives nothing either \(em its
top level has run, so the registration exists, but a runtime that is not
running executes no script, which is the same rule the timers are severed
under. They
.B do
come back if it is started again, which is where they differ from
.B set_interval
and
.BR set_timeout :
a subscription is registered from
.BR on_start ,
and
.B start
runs
.BR on_start ,
where a timer is armed at the script's top level and only a load runs that.
.TP
.B socketley.unsubscribe(subscription_id) \(-> bool
End one registration. Returns
.B true
when it removed a live one and
.B false
when the id names none \(em never issued, or already unsubscribed.
.IP
The id names one registration, not a channel, so two handlers on the same
channel can be ended independently. Safe to call from inside a subscriber's own
callback, which is how a one-shot subscriber is written; the removal takes
effect for the next message rather than in the middle of delivering this one.
.IP
Ending one explicitly matters while the runtime is up:
.B stop
ends them all, and
.B remove
and
.B reload\-lua
destroy the context they live in, but nothing does either of those on a
runtime that is simply serving.
Without ending them, a script that subscribes from a callback that runs more
than once \(em
.B on_tick
is the ordinary one \(em registers the same handler again on every call, and
each publish is delivered to every copy.
.TP
.B socketley.http(opts) \(-> table
Synchronous HTTP/HTTPS client.
.B WARNING:
Blocks the event loop thread \(em the wait is bounded, but it is not
asynchronous: the whole exchange completes before any other runtime in
the daemon is served again. Use only in
.BR on_start / on_stop
or low-frequency
.B on_tick
callbacks.
.PP
.I opts
table keys:
.nf
url         string   Request URL (http:// or https://)
method      string   HTTP method (default "GET")
body        string   Request body (optional)
headers     table    Extra headers e.g. {["Content-Type"]="application/json"}
timeout_ms  number   Timeout in milliseconds, 1..3600000 (default 5000)
.fi
.PP
A key that is present and of the wrong type or out of range comes back as
.B "ok = false"
with the reason in
.BR error ,
rather than being replaced by the default: `timeout_ms = "soon"` used to become
5000 and make the request anyway, on a number nobody chose. A key that is
absent keeps its default, which is not a mistake and is not reported.
.PP
Returns a table:
.nf
ok      bool    true if HTTP status 2xx
status  number  HTTP status code (e.g. 200, 404)
body    string  Response body
error   string  Error message on failure
.fi
.PP
.I timeout_ms
is one deadline for the whole exchange \(em connect, TLS handshake,
request and response are all measured against it \(em not a separate
timeout per operation. Name resolution is the exception: it runs before
the deadline is armed and has no bound of its own. A response is refused
once it exceeds 8 MB rather than buffered without limit.
.PP
HTTPS uses OpenSSL and validates the peer. The certificate chain is
verified against the system CA store, and the certificate must have been
issued to the host in the URL \(em a host name is matched against the
certificate's DNS names with partial wildcards refused, an address
literal against its IP SAN. TLS 1.2 is the minimum accepted protocol
version. Failure of either check aborts the request with the reason in
.IR error ,
and there is no option to disable it, so a service presenting a
self-signed or private-CA certificate must have that CA in the trust
store OpenSSL reads (overridable with
.B SSL_CERT_FILE
or
.BR SSL_CERT_DIR ).
If no CA store is available at all the call fails immediately with
.BR "no system CA store available" .
.SS Runtime Management API
Create, start, stop, and remove runtimes from Lua scripts:
.nf
socketley.create(type, name, config)  -- Create runtime
socketley.start(name)                 -- Start runtime
socketley.stop(name)                  -- Stop runtime
socketley.remove(name)                -- Stop + remove runtime
socketley.send(name, msg)             -- Send/broadcast to runtime
socketley.list()                      -- List all runtime names (table)
socketley.get(name)                   -- Get info table or nil
socketley.pipe(src, fd_a, src, fd_b)  -- Pair two connections of one server
socketley.pipe(src, fd, dst)          -- Route a connection to another server
.fi
.P
.B "Every call above answers " true " when it happened, or " false ", \(dqreason\(dq" " when it did not."
.RS
.PP
The reason is a sentence naming the runtime and what was wrong with the
request, so a script can tell the four situations apart that all used to answer
a bare
.BR false :
.PP
.nf
local ok, why = socketley.start("web")
--  false  there is no runtime called "web"
--  false  "web" is already running
--  false  "web" is failed, and a failed runtime cannot be started again ...
--  false  "web" could not be started; it is failed now, and the reason is
--         in the daemon's log        <- a held port, a TLS pair that is not one
.fi
.PP
This is the shape Lua itself uses \(em
.BR io.open ,
.BR os.remove ,
.B pcall
\(em so
.B "assert(socketley.start(n))"
prints the reason with no other change. Success returns ONE value, not
.BR "true, nil" .
.PP
.B What a second return value changes.
Lua truncates a multiple-return call to one value everywhere except three
places. Unaffected: assignment, \fBif not f() then\fR, \fBand\fR/\fBor\fR,
concatenation,
.BR tostring ,
comparison, and any argument position that is not the last. Affected:
.B print(f())
now prints two values,
.B "{ f() }"
builds a two-element table, and
.B g(f())
passes
.B g
two arguments where it passed one.
.RE
.PP
.B socketley.pipe
is two calls and the argument count says which. With four arguments it pairs
two connections of ONE server, both descriptors named and both used. With three
it hands the connection to another
.IR runtime ,
which decides what answers it \(em there is no destination descriptor in that,
so a fourth argument across two runtimes is refused rather than accepted and
discarded.
.P
.B Config table keys:
.BR port ,
.B config
(or
.BR lua ,
a script path),
.BR target ,
.B mode
(server only),
.B http
and
.B http_cache
(server only),
.BR group ,
.B on_parent_stop
("remove" or "stop"), and
.B start
(bool).
.B The table is validated and a refusal is a refusal:
a port outside 0..65535, a mode or policy that is not one of the listed values,
a key that is not on this list, a script that will not load, or a
.B start
that cannot bind, each returns
.B false
with the reason in the log and leaves no runtime behind. Lua-created runtimes
have parent-child ownership; port=0 creates internal-only servers that receive
forwarded messages only.
.PP
.B "Every call above works in a worker, which is where every runtime runs."
.RS
.PP
These calls act on the runtime map, and the runtime map lives in the daemon \(em
so a script written against them is running somewhere the map is not. All of
them work there anyway, and how they work differs:
.RS
.PP
.BR socketley.send ,
.BR .list ,
.B .get
and the four
.B socketley.cluster
calls are answered from a
.I replica.
The daemon pushes the worker a copy of the map and a snapshot of the cluster
view, and refreshes both when they change.
.PP
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop
and
.B .remove
.I change
the map, and a copy is not the thing to change. They are asked of the daemon
instead, over a socket the worker holds for exactly this, and they wait for the
answer \(em so
.B "if socketley.create(...) then"
tests what happened. The wait is what makes them honest and it is bounded: 9.95,
10.20 and 10.52 microseconds over three runs of 20000 for a call that does not
fork anything, and at most five seconds for one that does. They are made in
.B on_start
or on a rare control message, never per request, which is what makes a wait
affordable at all \(em see
.B socketley.http()
in the same table, which blocks the same loop for a network round trip.
.PP
The answers are the daemon's own, word for word what the same call says in the
daemon:
.B "there is no runtime called \(dqx\(dq" ,
.B "\(dqx\(dq is already running" ,
.BR "a runtime called \(dqx\(dq already exists" .
A script cannot tell from them which process it is running in, which is the
point.
.RE
.PP
.B "Two calls used to be refused there, and are not."
.B socketley.pipe
across two runtimes, and
.BR self.route() .
Their refusal said a pipe hands a file descriptor between runtimes and that no
channel existed to pass one over. Across two runtimes it hands no descriptor
over at all: the origin keeps the socket and relays, which needed a way to name
another runtime's connection rather than a way to move one. Both cross a process
boundary now, with no change to how either is called.
.PP
One refusal is left, and it is the one to read carefully if you meet it.
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop
and
.B .remove
raise in a worker that was forked
.I without
the socket they travel on \(em an older daemon, or one that could not create it.
That message says so and tells you to restart the daemon. It is not the
permanent refusal these four used to carry.
.PP
.B socketley.pipe
and
.B self.route()
were the last two refused in a worker, and are not any more. Their message said
a pipe hands a file descriptor between runtimes, that a descriptor cannot travel
in a snapshot of the map, and that it would need
.B SCM_RIGHTS
over a channel nothing had built. The mechanism it named
.RB ( shared/conn_transfer.h )
was real and the diagnosis was wrong: across two runtimes
.B socketley.pipe
does not hand a descriptor over at all. It is
.BR route_client () ,
which writes into the target's connection maps, runs the target's
.B on_connect
and then keeps the socket where it is and relays the bytes through it. What both
needed was a way to name another runtime's connection and reach it, which is
.BR shared/route_relay.h .
.PP
So they cross a process boundary now, and what changes for a script is nothing:
the same call, the same return value, the same target name. The origin stays in
the data path either way \(em that is what routing is \(em and a message from a
routed client reaches the target's
.B on_client_message
while the target's reply reaches the client through the origin.
.PP
The four\(hyargument form,
.BR "socketley.pipe(src, fd_a, src, fd_b)" ,
pairs two connections of one server and crosses nothing. It has to be called
from that server's own script, because only that process holds its connections.
.PP
When a runtime holding routed connections goes away \(em stopped, removed,
crashed or killed \(em every runtime on the other end of those routes is told to
let go, and the target's
.B on_disconnect
runs. That is done by the daemon rather than by the departing runtime, because a
killed worker runs no teardown at all.
.PP
.B unroute() ,
.B get_route()
and
.B owner_send()
read the runtime's own tables and always did.
.PP
Where the raise lands is the thing to plan for. At a script's top level it fails
the load, and a worker whose script did not load does not serve: the
.B create
is refused with a reason and the runtime is left
.BR failed .
Inside a callback it is caught and logged like any other callback error, and the
runtime carries on. In
.B on_start
that means a runtime that is up and serving with none of what
.B on_start
was for having happened \(em
.B ls
green, connections accepted \(em so the command that started it exits 1 and
names the callback and the reason. The runtime is left running either way; the
exit code and the message are how the two are told apart.
.PP
Everything that does not reach for the map works in a worker exactly as it does
in the daemon:
.BR socketley.log ,
.BR .http ,
.BR .json_encode / .json_decode ,
the timers,
.BR .subscribe ,
the whole
.B self
table, and every connection and request callback. See
.B "Out-of-process runtimes"
under
.B LIMITATIONS
for the full account.
.RE
.SS Cluster Introspection API
When the daemon runs in cluster mode (\fB\-\-cluster\fR), the
.B socketley.cluster
table provides read-only introspection into the full cluster topology.
All functions return empty tables when not in cluster mode, so scripts work in
both standalone and cluster setups without modification.
.PP
That last sentence holds for cluster mode being off, and it now holds in a
worker process too: the daemon pushes each worker a snapshot of the cluster view
before the frame that loads its script, and these four are answered from it.
.PP
One case is left. A daemon that is in no cluster has no view to push, and an
empty table is what a
.I successful
call returns for a daemon alone in one \(em so with no view installed the four
raise instead of answering, naming themselves. A daemon that
.I joins
a cluster after a worker's script has loaded leaves that script with the refusal
until it is reloaded.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.daemons() \(-> table
Returns an array of daemon info tables. Each entry has:
.nf
name      string   Daemon name (from \-\-name)
host      string   Advertised address, from gethostname(2), falling
                   back to the daemon name if that call fails
runtimes  number   Count of runtimes on this daemon
.fi
.IP
The local daemon is always the first entry. Remote daemons follow in discovery order.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.runtimes() \(-> table
Returns a flat array of all runtimes across every daemon in the cluster:
.nf
daemon       string   Name of the daemon hosting this runtime
name         string   Runtime name
type         string   "server", "client", "proxy", or "cache"
port         number   Listen port (0 = internal-only)
group        string   Group tag (empty string if unset)
state        string   "created", "running", "stopped", or "failed"
connections  number   Current connection count
.fi
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.group(name) \(-> table
Returns running members of the named group that have a valid port.
Each entry has:
.nf
daemon       string   Daemon name
host         string   Advertised address
port         number   Listen port
connections  number   Current connection count
.fi
.IP
Useful for building custom routing, health checks, or failover logic from Lua.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.stats() \(-> table
Aggregate cluster statistics:
.nf
daemons   number   Total daemon count (local + remote)
runtimes  number   Total runtime count across all daemons
running   number   Count of runtimes in "running" state
groups    table    Maps group name (string) \(-> member count (number)
.fi
.SS Cluster Event Callbacks
These optional callbacks fire when the cluster topology changes.
Events are detected during the 2-second scan/publish cycle, so callbacks
may fire up to 2 seconds after the actual change.
They fire only on a
.B running
runtime: a cluster callback is a callback, and a runtime that has been stopped
or has never been started executes no script. A batch of events that reaches a
runtime and then stops it ends there \(em the remaining events in that batch are
not delivered to it.
A daemon is considered stale and triggers
.B on_cluster_leave
after 10 seconds without a heartbeat update.
.TP
.B on_cluster_join(daemon)
Fired when a new remote daemon is discovered in the cluster directory.
.I daemon
is a table with
.B name
(string) and
.B host
(string) fields.
.TP
.B on_cluster_leave(daemon)
Fired when a remote daemon's heartbeat file becomes stale (>10s old) or is
removed from the cluster directory.
.I daemon
is a table with a
.B name
(string) field.
.TP
.B on_group_change(group, members)
Fired when the total member count of a group changes (a runtime joins or
leaves the group, or a daemon with group members appears/disappears).
Only fires on actual count changes, not on every scan cycle.
.I group
is the group name (string).
.I members
is an array of tables, each with
.BR daemon ", " host ", and " port
fields representing the current group membership.
.PP
Example \(em log topology changes and maintain a local routing table:
.nf
local backends = {}

function on_cluster_join(daemon)
    socketley.log("daemon joined: " .. daemon.name .. " at " .. daemon.host)
end

function on_cluster_leave(daemon)
    socketley.log("daemon left: " .. daemon.name)
end

function on_group_change(group, members)
    if group == "api" then
        backends = members   \-\- update routing table
        socketley.log("api group now has " .. #members .. " members")
    end
end

function on_start()
    \-\- Initial snapshot
    local stats = socketley.cluster.stats()
    socketley.log("cluster: " .. stats.daemons .. " daemons, "
                  .. stats.running .. " running runtimes")
end
.fi
.SH DASHBOARD
When
.B daemon.metrics_port
is set in the daemon configuration file, the metrics endpoint serves
multiple routes:
.TP
.B GET /
Live web dashboard. Auto-refreshes every 2 seconds. Shows all runtimes,
connection counts, throughput, and uptime in a browser-friendly view.
.TP
.B GET /metrics
Prometheus exposition format (existing). Suitable for scraping by
Prometheus, Grafana Agent, or similar monitoring stacks.
.TP
.B GET /api/overview
JSON object with high-level daemon statistics:
.nf
{
    "runtimes": 5,
    "connections": 142,
    "uptime_seconds": 86400,
    "throughput_in": 12345678,
    "throughput_out": 9876543
}
.fi
.TP
.B GET /api/runtimes
JSON array of all runtimes with name, type, port, state, connections,
and message/byte counters.
.TP
.BI "GET /api/runtime/" name
JSON object for a single runtime identified by
.IR name .
Returns 404 if the runtime does not exist.
.PP
The
.B dashboard
CLI command prints the dashboard URL:
.nf
$ socketley dashboard
Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
.fi
.SH SERVICE MESH
The proxy runtime includes service-mesh primitives that can be composed
to build sidecar proxies, API gateways, or resilient service-to-service
communication layers.
.SS Health Checks
Active health checking probes backends at a regular interval
.RB ( \-\-health\-interval )
using either a TCP connect or an HTTP GET
.RB ( \-\-health\-check ).
A backend is marked unhealthy after
.B \-\-health\-threshold
consecutive failures and is excluded from the load balancer pool.
A single successful check restores the backend.
.SS Circuit Breaker
The circuit breaker prevents cascading failures by tracking consecutive
errors per backend. It has three states:
.TP
.B closed
Normal operation. Requests are forwarded to the backend. Errors are
counted.
.TP
.B open
Triggered when errors reach
.BR \-\-circuit\-threshold .
All requests fail immediately without attempting a connection. After
.B \-\-circuit\-timeout
seconds, the circuit moves to half-open.
.TP
.B half-open
A single probe request is allowed through \(em one, not one per
selection: while that probe is outstanding the backend is unavailable to
everyone else. If it succeeds, the circuit returns to
.BR closed .
If it fails, the circuit re-opens.
.SS Response Deadline
A backend that accepts connections and then stops answering fails no
check: a TCP health probe connects to it successfully, and a request
that never completes is counted as neither an error nor a success, so
the circuit breaker has nothing to open on.
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
(default 2 seconds) bounds the wait for the first byte of a response and
records the expiry as a backend error, which is what puts such a backend
through the breaker like any other failure. The request is retried
elsewhere if
.B \-\-retry
allows it and answered
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
otherwise.
.PP
The deadline covers the handshake as well as the answer: a backend that
never completes its connect inside it is answered
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
too, rather than the client being closed without a response. A request
routed through an
.B @group
backend is charged to the member it actually reached, so a group member
that stalls is taken out of rotation by the same breaker as a listed
backend.
.PP
The deadline is on by default and only covers the wait for the first
byte, so a large or streaming response body is unaffected. An endpoint
that legitimately needs more than 2 seconds to start answering has to be
given a longer
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
or
.BR "\-\-backend\-timeout 0" ;
otherwise the proxy treats it as hung. It applies to HTTP mode only \(em
in TCP mode there is no request boundary to measure from.
.SS Request Framing
In HTTP mode the proxy is strict about how a request's header block is
framed, and refuses anything it and a backend could read differently.
These are the same rules a socketley server applies to a request it
receives directly, from one implementation \(em see
.B "Request Framing"
under
.BR "SERVER FLAGS" .
The proxy forwards bytes to a backend that parses them independently; if
the two disagree about where a request ends, the next request on the
same connection starts in the wrong place, which is request smuggling.
Normalising the request instead would mean guessing which way the
backend will read it, so the proxy declines to guess.
.PP
A request is answered
.B 400 Bad Request
and its connection is closed, without contacting any backend, when the
header block contains any of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a bare LF or a bare CR \(em every line must end with CRLF
.IP \(bu 2
whitespace between a field name and its colon
.RB ( "Content\-Length : 6" )
.IP \(bu 2
a continuation (obs\-fold) line, i.e. one starting with a space or tab
.IP \(bu 2
a field line with no colon
.IP \(bu 2
both
.B Content\-Length
and
.BR Transfer\-Encoding ,
or more than one
.B Content\-Length
field
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Content\-Length
that is not a whole decimal number
.RB ( "Content\-Length: 6x" )
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Transfer\-Encoding
whose final coding is not
.B chunked
.RB ( "Transfer\-Encoding: gzip" ,
.BR "Transfer\-Encoding: chunked, gzip" ,
or
.B chunked
listed twice) \(em such a request has no determinable body length at all
.RE
.PP
Every request on a connection is framed and checked on its own, including
one pipelined behind another. Only the body a request declares travels
with it: bytes past its
.B Content\-Length
are not body, and are held back and parsed as the next request rather
than forwarded. Pipelining still works \(em a second request sent before
the first is answered is served \(em but it is answered in turn, after
the same checks, and a smuggling attempt hidden behind a clean request is
refused exactly as it would be if it had been sent first. A request held
this way is subject to the same 10 second deadline as a half\-arrived one:
if the exchange in front of it never reaches an end this proxy can point
at \(em a backend whose response is framed in a way it cannot read \(em the
held request is answered
.B 408 Request Timeout
rather than left waiting. A
.B chunked
request is the one body this proxy cannot measure, because it never
de\-chunks; its body is forwarded as an opaque stream, so a request
pipelined behind a chunked one is framed by the backend alone.
.PP
Compliant clients emit none of these. A request whose header block is
still arriving is held until the terminating blank line, subject to the
16 KiB header limit
.RB ( "431 Request Header Fields Too Large" )
and a 10 second header deadline
.RB ( "408 Request Timeout" ).
.PP
There is a second, larger limit behind the header one, and it counts the
whole of what is buffered for an exchange rather than one header block: at
most 1 MiB is held for a connection whose request has already been
forwarded \(em the body it sends past its declared
.BR Content\-Length ,
and the requests pipelined behind it. Nothing drains that until the
response in front of it is finished, so a client that writes faster than
the backend answers reaches it on a well\-formed connection. It is
answered
.B 413 Request Entity Too Large
and the connection is closed. The one exception is the same one the
gateway statuses make: if part of the backend's response is already on
that client's wire, no status line is written behind it \(em it would land
at an offset that is not a message boundary \(em and the connection is
closed with the response left visibly truncated.
.PP
The header deadline starts at the first byte of a request and ends when
its header block does. It is fixed, is not configurable, and is
deliberately independent of
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout ,
which defaults to 0 (never) and so cannot be relied on to end a
half\-sent request: a client that sends a request line and then stops
sends no further bytes for the 16 KiB limit to count, and would
otherwise hold its connection, its descriptor and its 64 KB buffer for
as long as it liked. Neither an idle keep\-alive connection between
requests nor a slow request body is measured against it \(em only the
gap between a request's first byte and its terminating blank line.
.PP
The same strictness is applied to backend responses, but only to decide
whether the backend connection may be reused: a response whose length
cannot be established with certainty \(em because its header block was
split across reads, or was not strictly CRLF-framed, or because more
bytes arrived than the length it declared \(em is forwarded to
the client unchanged, and its connection is closed instead of returned
to the pool. A backend that answers
.B Connection: close
is not pooled either: it has said the socket is going away.
.PP
A
.B Connection: close
sent by the
.I client
does not do this. It is a hop-by-hop field about the client's own
connection to the proxy, so it is not forwarded upstream and it does not
end the backend connection: the proxy answers the request, returns the
backend socket to the pool, and closes the client connection as asked.
The field is a comma-separated list and is parsed token\-wise and
case\-insensitively, as it is on the server, so
.B "Connection: keep\-alive, close"
counts as close while a value that merely begins with those letters \(em
.B "Connection: clientinfo"
\(em does not.
A client that opens a connection per request \(em curl, a health checker,
any HTTP library used without a session \(em therefore costs one accept
and no backend connect, the same as a keep-alive client.
.PP
A connection already in the pool is checked before it is handed to the
next request, and one with unread data on it is closed rather than
reused. Nothing was asked of an idle pooled socket, so anything waiting
on it is something the backend sent unbidden \(em the tail of an answer
whose length was mis\-declared, or a second answer volunteered behind the
first. Reusing it would make those bytes the head of the next client's
response, so the connection is dropped and a fresh one opened. The proxy
logs this once per instance.
.PP
Reuse covers an
.B @group
backend as well as a listed one. A pooled connection is filed under the
endpoint it was opened to, never under the group, so it is only ever
handed back to a request routed to that same member; a member that leaves
the group has its pooled connections closed at once rather than left to
the idle sweep.
.PP
Reuse covers a
.B TLS
backend too, and the session is pooled with the descriptor rather than the
descriptor alone \(em a reused connection carries on the record layer it
already had, at the sequence number it had reached. A TLS connection carries
more than an address: a negotiated session, a peer identity verified against a
name, a client certificate that may have been presented, and a record layer
part way through a stream. The endpoint the socket is filed under answers three
of those, because it is what the name was pinned to before the handshake and
what the certificate was checked against; the client certificate and the trust
store belong to the runtime and are rebuilt on every start, and the pool is
emptied on every stop, so a pooled session can never outlive the configuration
that verified it.
.PP
What a pooled TLS socket cannot do is inherit an answer nobody asked for. A
cleartext socket with bytes waiting on it is destroyed rather than reused,
because a socket in the pool has been asked nothing since its last answer
completed. That test cannot be carried across a record layer unchanged: a
TLS peer that has said nothing still sends records \(em a session ticket, a key
update, a
.BR close_notify .
So the socket is drained into the session and the question is put to the record
layer instead of to the kernel: records that produce no application data are
the peer's own housekeeping and the connection is kept, and application data is
a response that belongs to a previous exchange and the connection is dropped.
The second case is logged once per runtime.
.SS Retries
When
.B \-\-retry
is set, failed requests are automatically retried on the next backend
in the load balancer pool. By default only idempotent methods are
retried; use
.B \-\-retry\-all
to include POST and PATCH.
.SS mTLS
Both legs can be encrypted.
.BR "\-\-tls \-\-cert \-\-key"
terminates TLS on the proxy's own port and
.B \-\-client\-ca
makes it demand a certificate from its callers;
.B https://
on a backend address, or any of
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.BR \-\-client\-key ,
takes the backend leg to TLS, with the chain and the endpoint's name verified
and a client certificate presented when one is configured. A full sidecar pair
looks like this:
.nf
socketley create proxy edge -p 8443 \\
    \-\-tls \-\-cert edge.pem \-\-key edge\-key.pem \\
    \-\-client\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-backend https://10.0.0.7:8443 \\
    \-\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-client\-cert edge.pem \-\-client\-key edge\-key.pem \\
    \-\-sidecar \-s
.fi
.PP
A TLS backend connection is returned to the connection pool and reused, so a
busy sidecar pays for a handshake per idle period rather than per request. The
pooled socket keeps its session, goes back only to the endpoint it was opened
to, and is dropped rather than reused if the backend has left application data
on it.
.B \-\-health\-check http
works against a TLS backend and probes it over TLS, verified to the same
standard as the data path \(em see
.B LIMITATIONS
for what the probe does and does not establish.
See
.B "mTLS Flags"
under
.B "PROXY FLAGS"
for what is verified and what cannot be switched off.
.SS Sidecar Deployment
The
.B \-\-sidecar
flag enables a production-ready default set: TCP health checks, circuit
breaker (threshold 5, timeout 30s), 2 retries, and graceful drain. Deploy
alongside your application:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -p 8080 -s
.fi
.PP
The proxy listens on port 8080 and forwards to the local application on
port 3000. Unhealthy backends are automatically removed, and the circuit
breaker prevents thundering-herd retries during outages.
.PP
.B \-\-sidecar
does not change
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
which is 2 seconds by default and charges its expiry to the circuit
breaker that
.B \-\-sidecar
enables. If the application can take longer than that to send its first
response byte, say so explicitly:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \-\-backend\-timeout 30 \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -p 8080 -s
.fi
.SH RUNTIME STATES
.TP
.B created
Initial state after creation.
.TP
.B running
Active and processing I/O.
.TP
.B stopped
Stopped but can be restarted.
.TP
.B failed
Setup failed, cannot recover.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a simple server (daemon is managed by systemd when installed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server myserver -p 9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Create client connecting to server:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client myclient -t 127.0.0.1:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Create HTTP proxy with load balancing:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy gateway -p 8080 \\
    --backend 127.0.0.1:9001,127.0.0.1:9002 \\
    --strategy round-robin -s
.fi
.PP
Server with bash output (messages to stdout):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server echo -p 9000 -b -s
# With timestamp and prefix:
socketley create server echo -p 9000 -bpt -s
.fi
.PP
Send message to runtime:
.PP
.nf
socketley send myserver "Hello from CLI"
socketley send myclient "Message to server"
.fi
.PP
Create persistent cache with maxmemory:
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache store -p 9000 \\
    --persistent /data/cache.bin \\
    --maxmemory 256M --eviction allkeys-lru -s
.fi
.PP
Create cache with RESP mode (Redis-compatible):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache redis-compat -p 6379 --resp -s
redis-cli -p 6379 SET mykey myval
redis-cli -p 6379 GET mykey
.fi
.PP
Server with connection limits and rate limiting:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 \\
    --max-connections 100 --rate-limit 1000 -s
.fi
.PP
View runtime statistics:
.PP
.nf
socketley stats myserver
# Output: name, type, port, connections, total_messages, bytes_in/out, ...
.fi
.PP
Restart a running runtime:
.PP
.nf
socketley reload myserver
# Stops and restarts the runtime
socketley reload *
# Restarts all running runtimes
.fi
.PP
Hot-reload Lua script:
.PP
.nf
socketley reload-lua myserver
# Re-loads the Lua script without stopping the runtime
socketley reload-lua *
# Hot-reloads Lua on all runtimes with scripts
.fi
.PP
Start/stop all runtimes:
.PP
.nf
socketley start *
# Starts all non-running runtimes
socketley stop *
# Stops all running runtimes
socketley remove *
# Stops all and removes everything
.fi
.PP
Multiple names and glob patterns:
.PP
.nf
socketley stop myserver myclient
# Stops both runtimes
socketley stop my*
# Stops all runtimes starting with "my"
socketley stats cache?
# Shows stats for cache1, cache2, etc.
socketley start myserver cache*
# Starts myserver and all cache-prefixed runtimes
socketley remove api-* test-*
# Removes all api- and test- prefixed runtimes
.fi
.PP
Interactive mode (live terminal session):
.PP
.nf
# Start server and enter interactive session
socketley create server echo -p 9000
socketley start echo -i
# Type messages to broadcast, see received messages
# Press Ctrl+C to detach (server keeps running)

# Attach to already-running cache
socketley start mycache -i
# Type cache commands: set foo bar, get foo, etc.
.fi
.PP
Interactive edit (opens JSON config in $EDITOR):
.PP
.nf
socketley edit myserver           # Opens config in editor
socketley edit myserver -r        # Opens editor, auto-reloads Lua after save
socketley edit myserver -p 9001   # Flag-based edit (direct)
.fi
.PP
Cache replication (leader-follower):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache leader -p 9000 -s
socketley create cache follower -p 9001 --replicate 127.0.0.1:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Encrypted server. The listener terminates TLS itself; everything above it \(em
line protocol, HTTP, WebSocket, RESP \(em runs on the decrypted stream, so
this same runtime serves
.B https://
and
.B wss://
on the one port:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server secure -p 9443 \\
    --tls --cert server.pem --key server-key.pem -s
curl --cacert server.pem https://localhost:9443/
.fi
.PP
Mutual TLS \(em a client must present a certificate signed by the given CA:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server mtls -p 9443 \\
    --tls --cert server.pem --key server-key.pem \\
    --ca trusted-clients.pem -s
.fi
.PP
Renewing a certificate: the files are read at start, so
.B reload
picks up a new one.
.PP
.nf
socketley reload secure
.fi
.PP
An incomplete configuration is refused rather than served in the clear:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server oops -p 9443 --tls --cert server.pem
# --tls: a TLS listener needs --key <file> to go with --cert
# exit status 1
.fi
.PP
Encrypted client. The dial is the mirror of the listener above: one flag turns
the transport on, and the peer is verified on both counts \(em the chain
against the trust store, and the certificate against the host in
.BR \-t .
.PP
.nf
# verify against the system trust store
socketley create client feed -t events.example.com:9443 --tls -s

# verify against a private CA instead
socketley create client feed -t 10.0.0.7:9443 \\
    --tls --ca internal-ca.pem -s

# and present a certificate of our own, for a peer that demands one
socketley create client feed -t 10.0.0.7:9443 \\
    --tls --ca internal-ca.pem \\
    --cert client.pem --key client-key.pem -s
.fi
.PP
A peer whose certificate does not verify costs the socket \(em the client
re-dials on its backoff and never falls back to cleartext. There is no
skip-verify flag: encryption without verification is protection against
somebody recording the wire and none at all against somebody sitting on it,
and the two look identical from the outside.
.PP
The same rules apply as on the listener. Half a certificate pair is refused,
a trust store without
.B \-\-tls
is refused, and
.B reload
is how a renewed client certificate is installed:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client bad -t host:9443 --tls --cert client.pem
# --cert: needs --key <file> as well -- a certificate with no key
# presents nothing
# exit status 1
.fi
.PP
Graceful shutdown (drain pending writes):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 --drain -s
socketley stop api   # Flushes write queues before closing
.fi
.PP
UDP server (fire-and-forget messaging):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server metrics -p 9000 --udp -b -s
echo "cpu.load 0.42" | socat - UDP:localhost:9000
.fi
.PP
UDP client:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client udpcli -t 127.0.0.1:9000 --udp -s
.fi
.PP
Create read-only cache (for shared lookups):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache lookup -p 9000 --mode readonly -s
.fi
.PP
Master mode server (one controller, many viewers):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server broadcast -p 9000 \\
    --mode master --master-pw secret -s
# Client A: sends "master secret" \(-> "master: ok"
# Client A: sends "hello" \(-> broadcast to all others
# Client B: sends "hello" \(-> silently dropped
.fi
.PP
Master mode with forwarding:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server ctrl -p 9000 \\
    --mode master --master-pw admin123 --send-to master -s
# Non-master messages forwarded to master as "[<fd>] msg"
.fi
.PP
WebSocket server (auto-detected, no flags needed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server ws -p 9000 -s
# Browser: new WebSocket("ws://localhost:9000")
# Raw TCP clients also work on the same port
.fi
.PP
Cache access through server:
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache db -p 9001 -s
socketley create server api -p 9000 --cache db -s
# Client sends: "cache set user:1 alice" \(-> "ok"
# Client sends: "cache get user:1" \(-> "alice"
# Client sends: "hello" \(-> broadcast as usual
.fi
.PP
Dynamic proxy with group-based backend discovery:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api1 -p 9001 -g api -s
socketley create server api2 -p 9002 -g api -s
socketley create proxy gw -p 8080 --backend @api -s
# Proxy routes to all running members of group "api"

# Add a new member dynamically (no proxy restart needed)
socketley create server api3 -p 9003 -g api -s

# Mix group and direct backends
socketley create proxy gw2 -p 8081 \\
    --backend @api,10.0.0.5:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Lua HTTP worker (auto-detected, no flags needed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 --lua handler.lua -s
# handler.lua:
# function on_http_request(req)
#     return {status=200, headers={["Content-Type"]="application/json"},
#             body='{"hello":"world"}'}
# end
# curl http://localhost:9000/api
.fi
.PP
Sidecar proxy with health checks and circuit breaker:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy sidecar -p 8080 \-\-sidecar \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -s
.fi
.PP
Proxy with HTTP health checks and retries:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy gateway -p 8080 \\
    \-\-backend 10.0.0.1:9000,10.0.0.2:9000 \\
    \-\-health\-check http \-\-health\-path /ready \\
    \-\-health\-interval 10 \-\-health\-threshold 5 \\
    \-\-retry 3 \-\-circuit\-threshold 10 -s
.fi
.PP
Proxy with mTLS on both legs. The listener terminates TLS and demands a
certificate from its callers; the backend leg dials TLS, verifies the chain
and the endpoint's name, and presents a certificate of its own:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy secure \-p 8443 \\
    \-\-tls \-\-cert proxy.pem \-\-key proxy\-key.pem \\
    \-\-client\-ca trusted\-clients.pem \\
    \-\-client\-cert backend\-client.pem \-\-client\-key backend\-client\-key.pem \\
    \-\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-backend https://10.0.0.1:9000 -s
# Backend connections are pooled with their sessions: a handshake per
# idle period, not per request.
# --health-check http probes over TLS and verifies the backend too.
.fi
.PP
TLS to the backend only \(em the proxy listens in cleartext on loopback and
encrypts the hop that leaves the machine:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy egress \-p 8080 \\
    \-\-backend https://api.example.com \-s
# Verified against the system CA store, name checked against the certificate.
# A private CA goes in --ca; there is no way to skip verification.
.fi
.PP
Open the live dashboard:
.PP
.nf
socketley dashboard
# Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
# Open in browser for live runtime view
.fi
.PP
Query dashboard API:
.PP
.nf
curl http://localhost:9100/api/overview
curl http://localhost:9100/api/runtimes
curl http://localhost:9100/api/runtime/myserver
.fi
.PP
Load complex configuration:
.PP
.nf
socketley --lua /etc/socketley/setup.lua
.fi
.SH CLUSTER MODE
.B "The cluster directory is as privileged as the daemons that read it."
A proxy with an
.BI @ group
backend picks its targets out of the files in that directory, so whoever can
write there decides where that traffic goes. Treat write access to it exactly
as you would treat the control socket: it belongs to the daemons and to nobody
else. A shared volume between containers you control is the intended
arrangement; a directory any other workload can write to is not.
.PP
Multi-daemon cluster discovery allows proxies to route to runtimes on remote
daemons. Each daemon publishes its state to a shared directory (typically a
Docker volume) every 2 seconds. Stale entries (heartbeat older than 10s) are
automatically excluded.
.PP
.B How it works:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Each daemon writes its state to
.IR dir / name .json
.IP 2. 3
Every 2 seconds, each daemon scans the directory for peer files
.IP 3. 3
Proxy
.B @group
resolution includes both local and remote group members
.IP 4. 3
On graceful shutdown, the daemon deletes its file
.RE
.PP
.B Restarting a node, and name conflicts:
.PP
A name is one node's.  Two daemons publishing one file would give every peer an
endpoint set belonging to neither, and split traffic for
.I api1
between two machines that each believe they are api1 \(em so a daemon that finds
its name held by a live entry is refused
.BR --cluster ,
and told so.
.PP
A node that was killed and restarted is not that case.  Every restart policy
this project documents \(em a container restart, a redeploy, systemd's
.BR "Restart=on-failure" ,
a crash loop \(em brings the node back in a second or two, well inside the 10s
threshold, and it meets its own entry still fresh.  It reclaims it.
.PP
The entry says who wrote it: the advertised host, the daemon's control socket
path, the kernel boot id, and the pid.  A fresh entry may be taken over only
when the first three are this daemon's own, and that is a proof rather than a
guess \(em a unix socket path admits one process at a time, and everything else
that reaches a daemon connects to that path instead of binding it.  So on one
machine, under one hostname, whoever holds the path
.I is
the daemon; an entry carrying our own three was written by a process that is
gone.  A different machine has a different boot id, and a fresh entry cannot
predate a reboot.  The pid is published for the operator and never used to
decide: a restarted container's pid namespace is new.
.PP
What this cannot tell apart is two containers on
.I one
host given the
.I same
hostname and both on the default socket path.  Their entries are identical, and
so is the address every peer would dial for either of them.
.PP
A daemon that could not join keeps serving \(em it is a working server that is
not in a cluster \(em and says so in three places: the exit status of the
command, an
.B ERROR
line in its log naming the holder, and every
.B socketley cluster
subcommand, which reports
.B "cluster mode is off"
with the reason instead of the same "not in cluster mode" a daemon that was
never given
.B --cluster
reports.  Clustering is never accepted and silently not applied.
.PP
.B Docker Compose example:
.PP
A complete, working one is
.IR examples/cluster/docker-compose.yml ,
and the same shape with assertions on it is
.IR test/docker/ .
Three things about it are not optional and were wrong in the example this
paragraph used to print:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.B seccomp.
Docker's default profile does not permit
.BR io_uring_setup (2),
.BR io_uring_enter (2)
or
.BR io_uring_register (2),
and socketley's whole I/O layer is a ring, so every node dies at startup with
.B "failed to init event loop"
under it.  Run the containers with a profile that allows those three syscalls
\(em during development,
.BR "security_opt: [seccomp:unconfined]" .
.IP \(bu 3
.B entrypoint.
The image's ENTRYPOINT is
.BR socketley ,
so a compose
.B command:
is passed to it as arguments.  A node that needs a shell has to say
.BR "entrypoint: [\(dq/bin/sh\(dq, \(dq-c\(dq]" ,
and give its script as a one\-element list \(em compose word\-splits a bare
string into an argv and everything past the first
.B &
is lost.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Readiness.
.B daemon --name
and
.B daemon --cluster
are configuration commands against a daemon that is already running, and they
auto\-start a detached one when the socket does not answer.  Sleeping for a
second before them is a race whose loss forks a second daemon and takes the
container down without a message.  Wait for the daemon's own
.B "daemon started"
line instead.
.RE
.PP
Alternatively, use a Lua config file:
.PP
.nf
\-\- /etc/socketley/setup.lua
daemon = { name = "api1", cluster = "/cluster" }
runtimes = {
    { type = "server", name = "web", port = 9001, group = "api", start = true }
}
.fi
.PP
.nf
socketley daemon &
sleep 1 && socketley --lua /etc/socketley/setup.lua
.fi
.PP
.B Inspecting the cluster:
.PP
.nf
socketley cluster ls
socketley cluster ps
socketley cluster group api
socketley cluster stats
socketley cluster watch
.fi
.PP
.B Stale detection:
.TS
l l l.
Scenario	Behavior	Recovery
_
Graceful shutdown	File deleted	Immediate
Container killed	Stale heartbeat	~10 seconds
Container restart	File overwritten	Immediate
.TE
.SH INSTALLATION
.SS Quick Install
.nf
sudo bash packaging/install.sh
sudo systemctl start socketley
.fi
.SS Debian Package
.nf
bash packaging/build-deb.sh 1.1.0
sudo dpkg -i socketley_1.1.0_amd64.deb
sudo systemctl start socketley
.fi
.SS Uninstall
.nf
sudo bash packaging/uninstall.sh
.fi
.SH STATE PERSISTENCE
Runtime configurations are persisted as JSON files. When a runtime is created,
its configuration is saved. When the daemon starts, all persisted runtimes are
restored. Runtimes that were running when the daemon stopped are auto-started.
.PP
This means runtimes survive daemon restarts and system reboots (like Docker
with restart-always policy).
.PP
State is saved after create, start, stop, remove, and edit commands.
The
.B was_running
field tracks whether a runtime should be auto-started on daemon startup.
.SS A State File Is A Secret
A runtime's record is not a note about the runtime, it
.I is
the runtime. It carries, in plaintext:
.TP
.B master_pw
the password given to
.BR "\-\-master\-pw" ,
verbatim.
.TP
.BR key_path ", " mesh_client_key
the paths of the TLS and mTLS private keys this runtime uses.
.TP
.B lua_script
the path of the handler the daemon loads, which runs with the daemon's
privileges \(em see
.B Handler Privileges
under
.BR "LUA CONFIGURATION" .
.PP
And the daemon restores every runtime from these files when it starts. So
whoever can
.I write
one chooses what the daemon runs at its next start, which is the same thing as
being able to send it commands.
.PP
Accordingly the daemon writes them
.BR 0600 ,
stated at creation rather than inherited from its umask, and the packaged state
directory is
.B 0750
owned by
.BR socketley:socketley .
Neither is configurable, and there is no supported reason to widen them: an
operator reaches this data through the CLI, over a socket with the same
group boundary, not by reading the directory.
.PP
.B "Earlier versions wrote these files 0666 & ~umask"
\(em 0644 under systemd's default, 0666 for a daemon started from a shell with
.BR "umask 0" .
Any local user could read a master password, and in the last case rewrite the
record. On its first start after an upgrade the daemon narrows every file it
finds in its state directory to 0600 and logs how many:
.PP
.nf
state: tightened 3 file(s) in /var/lib/socketley/runtimes to 0600 ...
.fi
.PP
It only ever narrows, it is idempotent, and it does not touch the directory
\(em that belongs to the installer. Seeing the line on every start means
something outside socketley is putting the modes back.
.PP
.B "A password that was exposed should be treated as exposed."
The repair closes the hole; it cannot un-read what was already readable. Rotate
any
.B \-\-master\-pw
that lived on a multi-user machine under an affected version, with
.B "socketley edit"
or by recreating the runtime.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Read at daemon startup; all are optional. The first selects which daemon this
is. The rest are tuning knobs whose defaults suit a general-purpose deployment
and only need changing for an unusual workload.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_BUF_SIZE
Bytes per cache read buffer. Default suits ordinary key/value traffic; raise it
only for a workload whose values routinely exceed it.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_BUF_COUNT
How many of those buffers a cache keeps.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_TTL_SWEEP_MS
How often a cache walks its expiry table, in milliseconds. Lower means expired
keys disappear sooner and the sweep runs more often; the sweep is on the event
loop thread, so it is not free.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
Which daemon you are, or which one you are talking to. Overrides the default
lookup
.RB ( /run/socketley/socketley.sock
when installed and privileged, otherwise
.BR /tmp/socketley.sock ).
.IP
No workload calls for setting it. A deployed node \(em a container or a host \(em has
a filesystem namespace to itself, so one daemon on the default path is the whole
arrangement, and that is what the systemd unit ships. Clustering does not use
this variable either: members publish themselves into a shared directory and
reach each other over network addresses, never over socket paths. See
.BR "CLUSTER MODE" .
.IP
What it is for is running several daemons inside
.I one
namespace, which is the development and test situation and nothing else. Two
checkouts on one workstation, or two test runs at once, are otherwise the same
daemon: whichever process binds the default path first owns it, and every later
one silently talks to that process instead of starting its own. Anything that
starts a daemon on the default path is claiming the identity the real daemon
runs under, so the test suite gives every test a socket of its own.
.IP
It selects the state directory as well as the socket, and it has to. Several
daemons in one namespace reading one state directory are not several daemons:
each restores everything in it at startup, including the others' runtimes, onto
ports the others are already serving, and each
.B remove
deletes a state file a daemon that is still running it depends on. See
.BR "WHERE STATE LIVES" .
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CPUS=\fImax\fR|\fI0\-3\fR|\fI0,1,2,3\fR
The set of CPUs the daemon's I/O thread may run on. Default
.BR max ,
which applies no pinning at all rather than a mask of the CPUs that happen to be
online now.
.IP
A CPU this machine does not have, or a set that comes out empty, is refused and
the daemon runs unpinned with a warning; it is never rounded up to
.BR max .
.IP
.BI "socketley daemon \-\-cpus " set
says the same thing on the command line and wins when both are given. There the
same impossible set is refused outright and the daemon does not start, because a
flag typed for this one run is a requirement rather than an inherited default.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_TTL_SWEEP_MS
How often a cache walks its expiry table, in milliseconds. Lower means expired
keys disappear sooner and the sweep runs more often; the sweep is on the event
loop thread, so it is not free.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
Which daemon you are, or which one you are talking to. Overrides the default
lookup
.RB ( /run/socketley/socketley.sock
when installed and privileged, otherwise
.BR /tmp/socketley.sock ).
.IP
No workload calls for setting it. A deployed node \(em a container or a host \(em has
a filesystem namespace to itself, so one daemon on the default path is the whole
arrangement, and that is what the systemd unit ships. Clustering does not use
this variable either: members publish themselves into a shared directory and
reach each other over network addresses, never over socket paths. See
.BR "CLUSTER MODE" .
.IP
What it is for is running several daemons inside
.I one
namespace, which is the development and test situation and nothing else. Two
checkouts on one workstation, or two test runs at once, are otherwise the same
daemon: whichever process binds the default path first owns it, and every later
one silently talks to that process instead of starting its own. Anything that
starts a daemon on the default path is claiming the identity the real daemon
runs under, so the test suite gives every test a socket of its own.
.IP
It selects the state directory as well as the socket, and it has to. Several
daemons in one namespace reading one state directory are not several daemons:
each restores everything in it at startup, including the others' runtimes, onto
ports the others are already serving, and each
.B remove
deletes a state file a daemon that is still running it depends on. See
.BR "WHERE STATE LIVES" .
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CPUS=\fImax\fR|\fI0\-3\fR|\fI0,1,2,3\fR
The set of CPUs the daemon's I/O thread may run on. Default
.BR max ,
which applies no pinning at all rather than a mask of the CPUs that happen to be
online now.
.IP
A CPU this machine does not have, or a set that comes out empty, is refused and
the daemon runs unpinned with a warning; it is never rounded up to
.BR max .
.IP
.BI "socketley daemon \-\-cpus " set
says the same thing on the command line and wins when both are given. There the
same impossible set is refused outright and the daemon does not start, because a
flag typed for this one run is a requirement rather than an inherited default.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_TTL_SWEEP_MS
Interval between periodic sweeps for expired cache keys, in milliseconds.
Default
.BR 500 .
This never affects correctness: an expired key is not served no matter when the
sweep runs, because every read path checks the expiry itself. It controls only
how quickly keys that are never accessed again release their memory.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_BUF_SIZE
Size in bytes of each buffer in a cache runtime's io_uring provided-buffer ring.
Default
.BR 4096 .
Larger buffers let one read completion carry more commands, but a read that
returns less than a full buffer still consumes the whole one, so the waste per
partial read grows with the size. Measured on the dev VM, raising this to 16384
cost 31% at 1 KB values and 8% at 4 KB values \(em the default is where it belongs
unless a specific workload shows otherwise.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_BUF_COUNT
Number of buffers in that ring. Default
.BR 512 .
Together with the size this bounds how much concurrent traffic the runtime can
absorb before the pool runs dry and reads fall back to a slower per-read path;
the pool costs count times size of memory per cache runtime.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_LUA_TIMEOUT_MS
How long one Lua callback invocation may hold the event\-loop thread, in
milliseconds. Default
.BR 10000 ;
.B 0
switches the bound off entirely and turns LuaJIT's trace compiler on with it,
which is worth about 2.4x on a callback that computes and about 1% on one that
does not. See
.BR "Callback Deadline" ,
which prices both halves of that trade.
.IP
This is the default for every script that does not declare its own
.B callback_timeout_ms
global; a script that does declare one overrides it. A value that is not a
whole number of milliseconds in 0\(en3600000 is refused with a warning and the
default is used.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_HTTP_REVALIDATE
Set to
.B 0
to switch off the response cache that plain
.B \-\-http
keeps, putting it back on an open, a read and a close per request. Any other
value, or the variable being unset, leaves it on. Read when the runtime starts,
so a
.B restart
picks up a change. What the server serves is the same either way \(em the cache
is revalidated on every request \(em so this exists for measurement and for
ruling the cache out when diagnosing something else.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_SPLICE
Forward
.B \-\-protocol tcp
proxy traffic with
.BR splice (2)
through io_uring instead of copying it through userspace. Unset (off) by
default; any value other than
.B 0
turns it on. Read once per process and inherited by worker processes.
.IP
Off because it is slower here, and not by a little.
.B IORING_OP_SPLICE
is prepped
.B REQ_F_FORCE_ASYNC
in the kernel, so every splice is handed to an io\-wq worker thread \(em a cost
per transfer that no way of submitting it avoids. Measured on loopback, one
connection, serial round trips through a proxy in front of an echoing backend,
round\-trip p50: 47.9\ \(mcs spliced against 26.3\ \(mcs copied at 128 B, 57.3
against 38.4 at 16 KB, and 394.5 against 350.5 at 256 KB. The copy path wins at
every size measured. It also costs a third less CPU \(em 0.53 cores against
0.86 at 128 B, which is 2.9 times the CPU per request for splice.
.IP
It is kept because what was measured is one machine and one shape of traffic.
Zero copy is a claim about memory bandwidth; the case it could still win is
bulk streaming across a real NIC on a box with less of it, and nobody has
measured that. A TLS listener or TLS backend never splices whatever this is set
to, and neither does a proxy with an
.B on_proxy_request
or
.B on_proxy_response
hook \(em the kernel moves those bytes without the script ever seeing them.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_NAPI_BUSY_POLL_US
Enable io_uring NAPI busy polling with this timeout in microseconds. Unset by
default. Trades CPU for lower receive latency on busy links.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CONFIG
Path to the daemon configuration file.
.SH FILES
.SS System Mode (installed via package)
.TP
.I /usr/bin/socketley
Binary.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/socketley.service
systemd unit file.
.TP
.I /run/socketley/socketley.sock
Unix domain socket for daemon IPC.
.BR 0660 ,
owned by
.BR socketley:socketley .
.TP
.I /var/lib/socketley/runtimes/
Per-runtime JSON configuration files. Directory
.BR 0750 ,
files
.BR 0600 ,
owned by
.BR socketley:socketley .
They carry master passwords and private-key paths; see
.B A State File Is A Secret
under
.BR "STATE PERSISTENCE" .
.TP
.I /etc/socketley/config.lua
Daemon configuration (name, cluster, log_level, metrics_port, cpus).
.TP
.I /etc/socketley/types/*.toml
Registered-type manifests, read at daemon startup; see
.BR "REGISTERED TYPES" .
.SS Development Mode (no system install)
.TP
.I /tmp/socketley.sock
Unix domain socket for daemon IPC.
.BR 0600 ,
owned by the user who started the daemon.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes/
Per-runtime JSON configuration files,
.BR 0600 .
.TP
.I ~/.config/socketley/config.lua
Daemon configuration (or set SOCKETLEY_CONFIG env var).
.TP
.I ~/.config/socketley/types/*.toml
Registered-type manifests, read at daemon startup; see
.BR "REGISTERED TYPES" .
.SS THE IPC SOCKET
Every
.B socketley
command travels over the socket above, and everything the daemon can do is
reachable through it: stopping a runtime, reading
.BR stats ,
rewriting a runtime's configuration with
.BR edit ,
and \(em since a
.B \-\-lua
handler runs with the daemon's privileges, see
.B Handler Privileges
under
.B LUA CONFIGURATION
\(em running arbitrary code as the user the daemon runs as.
.PP
So opening the socket is an administrative right, and the mode says so:
.PP
.nf
/run/socketley/socketley.sock   0660  socketley:socketley
any other path                  0600  the user who started the daemon
.fi
.PP
The packaged daemon runs as
.B socketley
and the operator does not, so the packaged socket is shared with one group and
membership of that group is how the right is granted:
.PP
.nf
sudo usermod \-aG socketley "$USER"     # then log in again
.fi
.PP
A daemon on any other path \(em a development daemon, or one started with
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
\(em keeps its socket to itself. That is owner\-only rather than
owner\-and\-group because a shared primary group is not unusual \(em
.B users
on several distributions \(em and there the daemon's group is every human on
the machine.
.PP
.B "This socket used to be 0666,"
world\-writable, on both paths. Any local uid could drive any daemon on the
machine, whoever it belonged to. After upgrading past that, a user who has
been running
.B socketley
against a system daemon without being in the
.B socketley
group will get
.PP
.nf
not permitted to open the daemon socket at /run/socketley/socketley.sock
.fi
.PP
and needs to be added to it. A user who is not added is not locked out of the
program: with the system socket unreachable the CLI falls back to development
mode and starts a daemon of that user's own, with that user's privileges and
that user's runtimes.
.PP
On a machine where two people use development mode at once, the second gets the
same message about
.I /tmp/socketley.sock
\(em one path, one owner, and it is no longer shared. That was never a
supported arrangement, it was a shared daemon nobody had asked for. Give each
their own:
.PP
.nf
export SOCKETLEY_SOCKET="/tmp/socketley-$USER.sock"
.fi
.PP
which also gives each a state directory of its own; see
.B WHERE STATE LIVES
above.
.SS WHERE STATE LIVES
The two paths above are where a daemon's runtimes live when it is serving the
default socket, which is every deployed daemon and every daemon that has never
heard of
.BR SOCKETLEY_SOCKET .
Nothing about that has changed or will.
.PP
A daemon on any
.I other
socket path gets a directory of its own, a sibling of the one above named after
that path: the leading slash is dropped and every character outside
.BR A-Za-z0-9._-
becomes an underscore.
.PP
.nf
SOCKETLEY_SOCKET unset          ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes
SOCKETLEY_SOCKET=/tmp/a.sock    ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes.tmp_a.sock
SOCKETLEY_SOCKET=/tmp/socketley.sock
                                ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes
.fi
.PP
Setting the variable to the path that would have been used anyway is the
default, not a second daemon, so a script that spells the default out keeps the
default directory. The name is readable rather than hashed on purpose: the
answer to "which of these is mine" should come out of an
.BR ls .
.PP
The daemon logs the directory it is using at startup
.RB ( "state directory: ..." ),
and that line is the authoritative answer. It is worth knowing where to find it
before you need it.
.PP
.B "Upgrading."
The first time a daemon uses one of these keyed directories, everything in the
shared directory it used to read is copied into it, once, and an INFO line says
how many runtimes were adopted and from where. The originals are left exactly
where they are, so an older binary rolled back onto the machine still finds what
it has always found. A
.I .adopted
file in the new directory records what happened and is what makes it a one-time
event \(em without it, an operator who later removed every runtime would find
them all back after the next restart.
.PP
.B "...but never out from under a running daemon."
Adoption is skipped entirely while a daemon is serving the default socket path,
because those runtimes are that daemon's and adopting them would mean binding
ports it is already serving. The check is a probe of the default path, and
anything answering there \(em daemon or not \(em is enough to decline.
.PP
This is not a rare corner. An operator debugging beside a running daemon reaches
it with one command, and the second bind
.I succeeds
under
.BR SO_REUSEPORT ,
so nothing but the port count would ever have reported it.
.PP
The decline is logged at
.B WARN
with both directory paths and what to do about it, because from the outside it
looks exactly like the runtimes having vanished. It writes no
.I .adopted
marker: "somebody is using these right now" is a fact about this minute rather
than a decision, so stopping the daemon on the default path and restarting this
one adopts them as normal.
.PP
.B "The failure this trades against."
A daemon started with a
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
it did not have last time finds no runtimes. On a development machine that is
correct: it is a different daemon and it owns nothing. It cannot happen to a
deployed node, because a deployed node runs on the default path with the
variable unset, and the default path is exempt from the rule for exactly that
reason. If it happens anyway, nothing has been destroyed \(em the other
directory is still on disk with everything in it, and starting the daemon with
the variable it had before brings it all back.
.PP
The alternative, one state directory for every daemon in the namespace, is not
the safe direction. Two daemons then bind each other's ports on boot, each
reports the other's runtimes as its own, and a
.B remove
sent to either deletes a state file the other is still serving \(em and there is
no undo for that.
.SS Daemon Configuration
The daemon config file is a Lua script that sets a
.B daemon
table:
.nf
daemon = {
    name = "node1",          -- this daemon's name in a cluster
    cluster = "/shared/dir", -- cluster directory; needs name
    log_level = "info",      -- debug, info, warn, error
    metrics_port = 9100,     -- Prometheus metrics HTTP endpoint
    cpus = "0-3"             -- where this daemon's I/O runs
}
.fi
.PP
.B config
is the same table under its older name and still works; a file naming both is
refused rather than merged.
.B name
and
.B cluster
are the two keys
.B "socketley \-\-lua"
can also set on a daemon that is already running \(em the other three are
settled before the ring exists, so they are read only from here, and a
.B \-\-cpus
typed on
.B "socketley daemon"
outranks the file.
.PP
When
.B metrics_port
is set, the daemon starts an HTTP endpoint that serves a live web dashboard
at
.BR "GET /" ,
Prometheus exposition format metrics at
.BR "GET /metrics" ,
and JSON API endpoints at
.BR "/api/overview" ", " "/api/runtimes" ", and " "/api/runtime/<name>"
(see
.BR DASHBOARD ).
Metrics include: runtimes total/running, connections total/active,
messages total, bytes in/out, and per-runtime breakdowns.
.SH C++ SDK
Socketley ships a three-tier modular C++ SDK in
.BR include/linux/socketley/ .
Tiers 1 and 3 are fully self-contained header-only files \(em copy them anywhere,
compile with
.BR "g++ -std=c++17" ,
no library needed. Tier 2 requires building from source.
.PP
.TS
l l l l .
Tier	Header	Use Case	Dependencies
_
1	socketley/control.h	Control a running daemon	None (POSIX)
2	socketley/{server,cache,proxy,client}.h	Embed the full engine	libsocketley_sdk.a
3	socketley/attach.h	Register with a daemon	None (Tier 1)
.TE
.SS Tier 1: Daemon Control (header-only)
.B socketley/control.h
provides full programmatic control over a running daemon through the same Unix
socket IPC that the CLI uses. Zero dependencies beyond POSIX.
.PP
.B "Result type:"
.nf
struct socketley::result {
    int         exit_code;  // 0=success, 1=bad input, 2=fatal, -1=connect fail
    std::string data;       // response body
};
.fi
.PP
.B "Runtime management functions"
(all in namespace
.BR socketley::ctl ):
.nf
command(cmd)               // raw command (glob patterns work)
command(socket_path, cmd)  // custom socket path
create(type, name, flags)  // "server", "client", "proxy", "cache"
start(name)                stop(name)
remove(name)               send(name, message)
ls()   ps()   stats(name)  show(name)
reload(name)               reload_lua(name)
edit(name, flags)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache string operations"
(direct IPC, no TCP connection needed):
.nf
cache_get(cache, key)           cache_set(cache, key, value)
cache_del(cache, key)           cache_exists(cache, key)
cache_incr(cache, key)          cache_decr(cache, key)
cache_incrby(cache, key, n)     cache_decrby(cache, key, n)
cache_append(cache, key, val)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache list operations:"
.nf
cache_lpush(cache, key, val)    cache_rpush(cache, key, val)
cache_lpop(cache, key)          cache_rpop(cache, key)
cache_llen(cache, key)          cache_lrange(cache, key, start, stop)
cache_lindex(cache, key, idx)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache set operations:"
.nf
cache_sadd(cache, key, member)  cache_srem(cache, key, member)
cache_sismember(cache, key, m)  cache_scard(cache, key)
cache_smembers(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache hash operations:"
.nf
cache_hset(cache, key, f, v)    cache_hget(cache, key, field)
cache_hdel(cache, key, field)   cache_hlen(cache, key)
cache_hgetall(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache TTL:"
.nf
cache_expire(cache, key, sec)   cache_ttl(cache, key)
cache_persist(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache pub/sub:"
.nf
cache_publish(cache, ch, msg)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache admin:"
.nf
cache_size(cache)               cache_memory(cache)
cache_keys(cache, pattern)
cache_flush(cache [, path])     cache_load(cache [, path])
.fi
.PP
Socket path resolution: checks
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
env, then
.BR /run/socketley/socketley.sock ,
then
.BR /tmp/socketley.sock .
Set the variable only to reach a daemon that is not the one for this namespace,
which in practice means a second daemon on a development machine; a deployed
node runs one daemon on the default path.
.PP
.B "Example: lifecycle management"
.nf
#include <socketley/control.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    auto r = socketley::ctl::create("server", "myapp", "-p 9000 -s");
    if (r.exit_code != 0) return 1;

    socketley::ctl::send("myapp", "Hello from C++!");
    auto stats = socketley::ctl::stats("myapp");
    printf("%s\\n", stats.data.c_str());

    socketley::ctl::stop("myapp");
    socketley::ctl::remove("myapp");
}
// g++ -std=c++17 myapp.cpp -Iinclude/linux -o myapp
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: fleet management with globs"
.nf
for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
    std::string name  = "api_" + std::to_string(i);
    std::string flags = "-p " + std::to_string(9000+i) + " -s";
    socketley::ctl::create("server", name, flags);
}
socketley::ctl::command("start api_*");    // start all
socketley::ctl::command("stats api_*");    // query all
socketley::ctl::command("stop api_*");     // stop all
socketley::ctl::command("remove api_*");   // remove all
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: cache operations"
.nf
socketley::ctl::create("cache", "store", "-p 6379 --mode admin -s");
socketley::ctl::cache_set("store", "user:1", "alice");
auto r = socketley::ctl::cache_get("store", "user:1");
printf("user:1 = %s\\n", r.data.c_str());  // "alice"
socketley::ctl::cache_del("store", "user:1");
socketley::ctl::cache_flush("store", "/tmp/store.bin");
.fi
.SS Tier 2: Embed Engine (build from source)
Embed the full io_uring event loop and all four runtime types in your own binary.
Maximum performance with no IPC overhead. Requires
.B libsocketley_sdk.a
and link flags
.BR "-luring -lssl -lcrypto [-lluajit]" .
.PP
Headers:
.B socketley/core.h
(event_loop, runtime_manager, enums),
.B socketley/server.h ,
.B socketley/client.h ,
.B socketley/proxy.h ,
.B socketley/cache.h .
.PP
Define
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_LUA
to build without LuaJIT (omit
.B -lluajit
from link flags).
.PP
.B "High-Level Wrapper Classes (recommended)"
.PP
The
.B socketley::server\fR, \fBsocketley::client\fR, \fBsocketley::proxy\fR, and \fBsocketley::cache
classes wrap the raw engine API into a fluent, chainable interface.
All boilerplate (event loop, runtime manager, signal handling) is internal.
.PP
.B "socketley::server"
.nf
server(uint16_t port)

// Config (all return server& for chaining)
tls(cert, key)         // terminates TLS on this listener
client_ca(ca)          // mutual TLS on it: clients must present a certificate
                       // (tls_ca(ca) is the older spelling; it also sets tls)
max_connections(n)     rate_limit(r)
global_rate_limit(r)   idle_timeout(s)       lua(path)
mode(m)                udp()                 http_dir(path)
http_cache()           upstream(addr)        master_pw(pw)
drain()                group(g)              tick_interval(ms)

// Callbacks (return server& for chaining)
on_start(void())                             on_stop(void())
on_connect(void(int fd))                     on_disconnect(void(int fd))
on_message(void(int fd, string_view msg))    on_tick(void(double dt_ms))

// Actions
send(fd, msg)       broadcast(msg)      disconnect(fd)
peer_ip(fd)         clients()           multicast(fds, msg)
set_data(fd,k,v)    get_data(fd,k)

// Lifecycle
start()   stop()

// Escape hatches (raw engine access)
instance() -> server_instance*
manager()  -> runtime_manager&
loop()     -> event_loop&
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::client"
.nf
client(string_view host, uint16_t port)

// Config
tls()              // dial over TLS; the host from the constructor is pinned
                   // and the chain verified against the system trust store
tls_ca(ca)         // verify against that CA instead of the system store
client_cert(c, k)  // the certificate this client presents when the peer asks
                   // for one - the client half of mutual TLS; needs tls()
reconnect(max)     mode(m)            udp()
lua(path)          tick_interval(ms)

// Callbacks
on_start    on_stop    on_connect    on_disconnect
on_message(void(string_view msg))   on_tick

// Actions
send(msg)

// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::proxy"
.nf
proxy(uint16_t port)

// Config
backend(addr)      strategy(s)        protocol(p)
tls(cert, key)     // terminates TLS on this proxy's listener
client_ca(ca)      // mutual TLS on it: clients must present a certificate
backend_ca(ca)     // trust store for the backend leg; turns that leg to TLS
client_cert(c, k)  // the certificate presented to a backend that asks
max_connections(n) idle_timeout(s)
lua(path)          group(g)

// Callbacks: on_start  on_stop
// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::cache"
.nf
cache(uint16_t port)

// Config
persistent(path)   max_memory(bytes)   eviction(policy)
resp()             mode(m)
tls(cert, key)     // NOT IMPLEMENTED - refused, no handshake here
max_connections(n) idle_timeout(s)     replicate(target)
lua(path)          group(g)

// Callbacks: on_start  on_stop
// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "Limitations:"
One
.B start()
per process (the signal handler points to one event_loop).
.B start()
blocks until SIGINT/SIGTERM. For multi-runtime or async patterns, use the raw API
via the escape hatches or directly.
.PP
.B "TLS: on a server and a proxy only."
.B server().tls()
and
.B proxy().tls()
terminate TLS on that runtime's listener, and
.BR proxy().backend_ca() ,
.B proxy().client_ca()
and
.B proxy().client_cert()
configure the proxy's two legs, exactly as the CLI flags do \(em
.B start()
goes through
.B runtime_manager::start
and reaches the same gate. On a
.B client
or
.B cache
builder the same entry points still store their arguments and are read by
nothing, and there
.B start()
refuses rather than listening in cleartext. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.PP
.B "Example: echo server (3 lines)"
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    socketley::server srv(9000);

    srv.on_connect([](int fd) {
        printf("client %d connected\\n", fd);
    });
    srv.on_message([&](int fd, std::string_view msg) {
        srv.send(fd, "echo: " + std::string(msg));
    });
    srv.on_disconnect([](int fd) {
        printf("client %d disconnected\\n", fd);
    });

    srv.start();
}
// g++ -std=c++23 echo.cpp $(sdk/socketley-config --cflags --libs)
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: server with Lua scripting"
.nf
int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    socketley::server srv(9000);
    srv.lua(argv[1]);
    srv.start();
}
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: TCP proxy"
.nf
socketley::proxy px(8080);
px.backend("127.0.0.1:9001")
  .backend("127.0.0.1:9002")
  .protocol(protocol_tcp)
  .strategy(strategy_round_robin);
px.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: Redis-compatible cache"
.nf
socketley::cache c(6379);
c.persistent("/var/data/store.dat")
 .resp()
 .max_memory(256 * 1024 * 1024)
 .eviction(evict_allkeys_lru);
c.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: client with auto-reconnect"
.nf
socketley::client cli("127.0.0.1", 9000);
cli.reconnect(0);  // 0 = infinite
cli.on_connect([&](int) { cli.send("hello"); });
cli.on_message([](std::string_view msg) {
    printf("%.*s\\n", (int)msg.size(), msg.data());
});
cli.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Raw Engine API (advanced)"
.PP
For multi-runtime processes or custom event loop control, use the raw types directly.
The wrapper classes use this API internally.
.PP
.B "Core pattern:"
create event_loop, create runtime_manager, create runtimes, configure, run, loop.
.PP
.B "C++ callbacks"
(alternative to Lua scripts, set on any runtime):
.nf
set_on_start(function<void()>)
set_on_stop(function<void()>)
set_on_connect(function<void(int fd)>)
set_on_disconnect(function<void(int fd)>)
set_on_client_message(function<void(int fd, string_view)>)
set_on_message(function<void(string_view)>)
set_on_tick(function<void(double dt_ms)>)
.fi
.PP
.B "Common runtime setters:"
.nf
set_port(uint16_t)            set_event_loop(event_loop*)
set_runtime_manager(mgr*)     set_cache_name(string_view)
// set_tls / set_cert_path / set_key_path / set_ca_path are honoured
// on a server runtime and refused on every other type (see LIMITATIONS).
set_max_connections(uint32_t)
set_rate_limit(double)        set_idle_timeout(uint32_t)
set_log_file(string_view)     set_group(string_view)
set_drain(bool)               load_lua_script(string_view)
.fi
.PP
.B "Server-specific:"
.nf
set_mode(mode_inout|mode_in|mode_out|mode_master)
set_udp(bool)          set_http_dir(string_view)
broadcast(msg)         send_to(fd, msg)
disconnect(fd)         peer_ip(fd)
clients()              multicast(fds, msg)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache-specific:"
.nf
set_mode(cache_mode_readonly|readwrite|admin)
set_persistent(path)   set_max_memory(bytes)
set_resp_forced(bool)  set_replicate_target(host:port)
.fi
.PP
.B "Proxy-specific:"
.nf
set_protocol(protocol_http|protocol_tcp)
set_strategy(strategy_round_robin|random|lua)
add_backend(addr)      clear_backends()
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: multi-runtime with raw API (server + cache)"
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <socketley/cache.h>

// ... (event_loop + runtime_manager setup as above)

mgr.create(runtime_cache, "store");
auto* cache = static_cast<cache_instance*>(mgr.get("store"));
cache->set_port(6379);
cache->set_mode(cache_mode_admin);
cache->set_runtime_manager(&mgr);
cache->set_event_loop(&loop);

mgr.create(runtime_server, "api");
auto* srv = static_cast<server_instance*>(mgr.get("api"));
srv->set_port(9000);
srv->set_cache_name("store");  // clients can "cache set k v"
srv->set_runtime_manager(&mgr);
srv->set_event_loop(&loop);

mgr.start("store", loop);
mgr.start("api", loop);
loop.run();
.fi
.SS Cross-Platform SDK (Windows / macOS)
Header-only TCP clients for connecting to Socketley runtimes from non-Linux platforms.
One copy, in
.BR include/portable/socketley/ ,
reached as
.B <socketley/ws_client.h>
from any of
.IR include/linux ", " include/darwin " or " include/windows .
.PP
.B socketley/ws_client.h
\(em WebSocket (RFC 6455) + raw TCP client for server runtimes.
Embedded SHA-1 and Base64 (no OpenSSL). Modes:
.BR ws_mode::websocket " and " ws_mode::raw_tcp .
.PP
.B socketley/cache_client.h
\(em text protocol client for cache runtimes. Supports all cache commands
(get, set, del, lpush, sadd, hset, expire, publish, subscribe, etc.).
Returns
.B cache_result
with
.IR ok ", " value ", " values ", " integer
fields.
.PP
Build with C++17, no external dependencies:
.RS
.nf
# MSVC
cl /std:c++17 /I include\\windows app.cpp

# clang++ (macOS)
clang++ -std=c++17 -I include/darwin app.cpp -o app

# g++ (Linux)
g++ -std=c++17 -I include/linux app.cpp -o app
.fi
.RE
.SS Tier 3: Daemon Attach (header-only)
.B socketley/attach.h
registers your own process as a runtime with a running daemon.
After attaching, your process shows up in
.BR "socketley ls" ,
.BR "socketley stats" ,
and
.BR "socketley ps" .
Fully self-contained (builds on control.h). Auto-deregisters on
.BR exit() .
.PP
.B "API:"
.nf
bool daemon_attach(name, type, port)  // register, auto-detach on exit
void daemon_detach(name)              // explicit deregister
void daemon_detach()                  // deregister attach'd name
.fi
.PP
.B "Example:"
.nf
#include <socketley/attach.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    // Start your own TCP server (any framework)
    // ...bind, listen on port 8080...

    if (socketley::daemon_attach("myservice", "server", 8080))
        printf("registered with daemon\\n");
    else
        printf("daemon not running (standalone mode)\\n");

    // Your accept loop here...
    // atexit() automatically calls daemon_detach()
}
// g++ -std=c++17 myapp.cpp -Iinclude/linux -o myapp
.fi
.PP
.B daemon_attach()
returns false when the process may not open the daemon's IPC socket, which is
the same permission every
.B socketley
command needs \(em see
.B THE IPC SOCKET
under
.BR FILES .
A program that runs as a different user from the daemon must be in the
.B socketley
group to register with it. False is not an error to abort on: the example above
is the intended shape, the program runs standalone and is simply not in the
daemon's inventory.
.SS Mixed: Engine + Daemon Attach (Tier 2 + 3)
Embed the full engine for maximum performance, and register with the daemon
for fleet monitoring. Best of both worlds.
.PP
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <socketley/attach.h>

socketley::server srv(9000);
srv.on_message([&](int fd, std::string_view msg) {
    srv.send(fd, msg);
});

// Register with daemon so socketley ls/stats can see it
socketley::daemon_attach("app_srv", "server", 9000);

srv.start();  // daemon sees the runtime
.fi
.PP
.B "When to use each tier:"
.PP
.TP
.B "Tier 1"
Orchestration scripts, deployment tools, monitoring, CI/CD.
.TP
.B "Tier 2"
Custom daemons, game servers, IoT gateways, performance-critical services.
.TP
.B "Tier 3"
Legacy services, third-party processes, fleet visibility for external apps.
.TP
.B "Mixed"
Embedded engine with daemon monitoring \(em maximum performance with fleet management.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.SS WebSocket and other protocol upgrades
A proxy in
.B http
mode relays a connection that has switched protocols, which is what makes a
.B ws://
client work through it. What that consists of, and what it deliberately is
not:
.PP
The upgrade is negotiated by the two endpoints and by nothing in between. The
proxy forwards the request's
.B Upgrade
and
.B Connection
headers unchanged instead of stripping them as hop-by-hop, relays the
.B 101
verbatim, and computes no part of it \(em there is no
.B Sec\-WebSocket\-Accept
calculation here, no check that the key matches, and no opinion about the
protocol named. It keys off the 101 and not off the token, so any upgrade two
endpoints agree on is relayed the same way.
.PP
.B "Frames are never parsed."
After the 101 the connection carries opaque bytes. The proxy does not know
where one frame ends and the next begins, so there is no fragmentation
handling, no ping or pong answered on a backend's behalf, no
.B permessage\-deflate
negotiation, and no per-message limit \(em
.B \-\-max\-connections
still counts connections, and nothing counts frames. The
.B on_proxy_request
and
.B on_proxy_response
callbacks still see these bytes, but they see whatever a read delivered rather
than whole frames, and a callback that rewrites them corrupts the stream: a
frame carries its own length and a client-to-server frame is masked.
.PP
.B "A tunnelled backend connection is never pooled."
There is no message boundary to return it at, so one WebSocket costs one
backend TCP connection for its whole life, however long that is. A proxy in
front of many long-lived WebSocket clients holds that many backend sockets.
.PP
The deadlines behave as follows.
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
bounds the wait for the backend's first response byte, and the 101 is that
byte \(em so it applies to the handshake and stops applying the moment the
switch happens. A tunnel is not measured against it and cannot be answered
.B 504
for being quiet, which is the point: nothing may arrive for minutes.
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
still applies, and on such a connection it measures bytes in
.I either
direction rather than only what the client sends: a stream the backend is
feeding is not idle. A tunnel that is genuinely silent both ways for longer
than the timeout is closed by it, as any other connection would be.
.PP
For the circuit breaker a tunnel is one successful response \(em the 101 \(em
and nothing more; a session ending is a connection closing and is not charged
to the backend. In
.B stats
the bytes relayed afterwards are counted in
.B bytes_in
and
.BR bytes_out ,
and
.B total_messages
counts the reads that carried them rather than frames or requests, so a proxy
carrying WebSocket traffic reports figures that do not correspond to messages
in either protocol's sense.
.PP
A proxy in
.B \-\-protocol tcp
mode is unaffected by any of this: it was already a byte relay and never looked
at a status line.
.SS What can stop the event loop
One thread serves every runtime in a process, so a call that waits stops all of
them. Six places do, and they are named here rather than left to be found: the
first of them already carried a warning at its own entry and the other five
carried none.
.PP
Measured where measuring was possible, on a Lua echo server and a static file
server under load. A share of a worker's CPU is what a cost looks like on a
healthy machine; what none of these figures say is what the same call costs when
the disk is busy or the resolver is down, which is the case they matter for.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley.http()
\(em the whole exchange, per call from a script: name resolution, the connect
and the transfer, bounded only by the callback's remaining budget. Its own entry
says to use it in
.B on_start
and nowhere else, which is the honest way of saying the capability is not usable
where it is wanted.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR open (2)
and
.BR fstat (2)
in the static file path, per request that is not answered from
.BR \-\-http\-cache .
Measured at about 4.8 per cent of the worker \(em 1.7 in the kernel's open path
and 3.1 in
.BR statx ,
which is what glibc turns an
.B fstat
into.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
on a client's reconnect, a server's upstream, a cache's
.B \-\-replicate
and a proxy's setup. Not on any request path \(em the proxy resolves at setup
for exactly this reason \(em but a reconnect against a resolver that is not
answering holds the loop for as long as the resolver takes. There is no
asynchronous form of it in glibc, so this one is a design decision and not a
repair.
.IP \(bu 2
The mutation journal's flush, once per
.B \-\-persistent\-interval
while anything is buffered. A write to the page cache and nothing more on a
healthy disk; a write to a full or slow one is the whole interval's worth of
clients waiting.
.IP \(bu 2
The cache's snapshot and its
.BR fsync ,
at teardown rather than periodically \(em which is why it is last of the disk
ones and not first. A stop takes as long as the dump takes.
.IP \(bu 2
The state file's write and
.BR fsync ,
on a runtime's state changing. Rare, and on the daemon's loop rather than a
worker's, so what it holds up is every runtime at once.
.RE
.PP
.B "What is NOT on this list, having been suspected and measured."
A static file server spends 7.3 per cent of its worker in the kernel clearing
pages, which reads like an allocation churning. It is not: the same server
answering half a million requests took fourteen minor page faults in total, so
the pages are the kernel's own, for the 1.7 GB a second it is putting on the
wire. The malloc and free around each file buffer are served from the heap and
never reach it.
.SS Out\-of\-process runtimes
A runtime of any type runs in its own supervised worker process. There is no
longer an alternative and no flag to ask for one. What that costs is worth
stating plainly.
.PP
.B "The script runs in the worker and nowhere else."
An out\-of\-process runtime exists twice: the worker that owns the listening
socket and serves every byte, and a placeholder in the daemon that carries its
name, port, group and state so
.BR ls ,
.B stats
and the endpoint table have something to answer with. The script belongs to the
worker. The placeholder compiles it \(em so a file that does not parse is still
refused by
.B create
on the spot, rather than becoming "the worker exited and the runtime is failed"
\(em and then discards it without executing a line.
.IP
It used to load it as well, so every top\-level line ran twice, and the two
copies did not do the same thing: the daemon's placeholder holds the runtime
manager and the worker does not, so a top\-level
.B socketley.create()
created a runtime from inside the daemon while the identical line in the worker
was refused.
.IP
A script's
.B routes
declaration is not given up with it, though it was until the worker learned to
send it. The declaration is produced by running the script, and only the worker
runs it; the placeholder had none, so
.B publish_endpoints
published none and a gateway in front of such a runtime forwarded every method
to it instead of refusing the undeclared ones at the edge with a 405 \(em
enforcement that quietly stopped existing, which is the worst way for a gate to
fail. The worker now encodes the declaration into a
.B routes
frame on the control socket it already holds, the supervisor decodes it onto the
placeholder, and the daemon publishes the worker's copy in preference to a local
one. A
.B reload\-lua
on such a runtime waits for the new declaration before republishing, so the
gateway is never enforcing the old script's methods against the new script's
handler. The declaration is a handler's feature rather than a proxy's, which is
what made this matter the moment every type got its own process by default.
.PP
.B "create waits for the worker's verdict."
Compiling the script in the placeholder catches a file that does not parse, and
nothing else: the rest of the configuration is judged in the worker, where the
script actually runs, the port is actually bound and the TLS pair is actually
read. So
.B create
does not answer when it has forked a process \(em it waits until that process
says whether it can serve what it was handed.
.IP
Three things end the wait. The worker reports that it is serving, and
.B create
exits 0. The worker refuses, or starts and cannot bind, resolve its backend or
load its certificates, and
.B create
exits 1 with the reason in the daemon's log \(em the runtime is left
.B failed
where
.B ls
shows it, rather than being removed, so there is still something to ask about.
Or neither answer arrives within two seconds, and
.B create
exits 2 saying so; the runtime stays under supervision and
.B ls
reports whatever it settles on.
.IP
The bound is a backstop, not a budget. A worker that has to compile a script,
build its ring and bind a listener reports back in about 20 ms, and a refusal
comes back sooner because the child stops at the script; two seconds is only
reached by a worker that has wedged.
.B create
never blocks indefinitely on a child.
.PP
.B "A runtime in a worker can still ask another runtime by name."
.B \-\-cache
works across the boundary in both directions. A request for a name the asking
process does not hold goes out on the control socket; the daemon holds the
whole map and is the only process that can route, so it looks the name up,
posts the request to whatever answers for it \(em in the daemon or in another
worker \(em and writes the answer back. Neither the asking runtime nor the
client on the other end of the socket can tell which arrangement served it.
A name nobody answers to is refused, with the same
.B "error: no such runtime"
an in\-process runtime gives, rather than being dropped in silence.
.PP
.B "The cross\-runtime management API is refused inside a worker."
A worker is a separate process holding one runtime and no runtime manager, so
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop ,
.BR .remove ,
.BR .send ,
.BR .list ,
.BR .get ,
.B .pipe
and the whole
.B socketley.cluster
table have no inventory to act on. They are not offered, and they do not quietly
answer nothing: each raises a Lua error naming the call, the reason and the way
out. Every one of them used to return a false, a nil or an empty table \(em
which is exactly what a
.I successful
call returns on a daemon with nothing in it, so neither the script nor the
operator could tell the two apart.
.IP
Called from a callback, the refusal is reported by whatever ran it: a filter
that raised has not said "carry on", so the request is answered
.B 500
and the message goes to the log. Called at the script's top level, the script
does not load \(em and a worker whose script did not load does not serve. It
exits saying this is a configuration it will never accept, and the supervisor
leaves the runtime
.B failed
rather than forking five more copies of the same refusal. It used to start
anyway and relay with none of the filters, routes or callbacks that were
written, behind a single warning.
.IP
Everything that does not reach for the runtime map works normally:
.BR socketley.log ,
.BR .http ,
.BR .json_encode / .json_decode ,
the timers,
.BR .subscribe ,
the
.B self
table, and the connection and request callbacks.
.IP
.B "self.route() crosses the boundary, and this section used to say it could not."
It names another runtime, so it was grouped with the calls that reach for the
map, and its refusal told a reader that "a connection cannot be routed out of a
worker
.I today"
and that naming a runtime and handing it an open connection were separate
mechanisms of which only the first existed.
.IP
That was measured and correct at the time \(em with two servers at the
out\-of\-process default it answered
.BR false ,
the target counted no connection and its
.B on_connect
never fired \(em and the conclusion drawn from it was wrong.
.BR route_client ()
never needed a descriptor to travel. It needed the target's connection tables,
which it reaches by
.B conn_ref
now, through
.BR shared/route_relay.h .
The origin keeps the socket and relays the bytes, exactly as it does when both
runtimes are in one process.
.IP
.BR unroute() ,
.B get_route()
and
.B owner_send()
answer from this runtime's own tables, and always did.
.PP
.B "Cross\-runtime pub/sub reaches a worker."
A cache's
.B PUBLISH
is delivered to a worker's
.B subscribe
callbacks over its control socket, and the daemon no longer runs them against
the placeholder it keeps for the runtime. It used to: the placeholder loads the
same script, so its copy of the callback fired \(em inside the daemon, attached
to an instance with no connections \(em while the process actually relaying the
traffic heard nothing. Delivery is one\-way and best\-effort, like every other
frame on that socket. A publish is an event and not a state, so a frame dropped
because the child is not reading is not re\-sent, and one that arrives before
the worker has been configured is discarded rather than replayed at a subscriber
that did not exist when it happened.
.PP
.B "Interactive mode (start \-i) is not offered for a proxy, on either path."
Interactive mode is a stream: what is typed reaches a server's clients, a
client's server, or a cache's command parser. A proxy relays between a client
and a backend and speaks for neither, so there is nothing for typed input to
mean, and offering it would mean inventing a meaning rather than exposing one.
The command refuses and exits 1
.I without starting anything
\(em it used to start the proxy, persist
.BR was_running ,
and then report the failure, leaving a listener up and a state file that would
bring it back on the next daemon restart.
.PP
.B "A worker's figures are read when they are printed."
.BR connections ,
.BR total_connections ,
.BR total_messages ,
.B bytes_in
and
.B bytes_out
for an out\-of\-process runtime are summed out of a small shared\-memory region
at the instant the command prints them. Each worker stores its own line into
that region once per turn of its event loop; the daemon adds the lines up. There
is no round trip, nothing waits on a child that may be wedged, and there is no
interval between the reading and the printing for a figure to go stale in.
.PP
Every figure is therefore identical on both paths, which is the property the
split exists to have. Earlier releases printed
.B figures:pushed
and
.BI figures_age_ms: n
here, because a worker pushed its counters on a quarter\-second timer and the
difference was real. Those lines no longer exist and nothing replaces them:
there is no age left to report.
.PP
The part of
.B stats
that is not a summable counter \(em a cache's key count, a proxy's healthy
backends, the ring's granted polling mode \(em is still computed inside the
worker, because it is a walk of tables that exist only there. It crosses the
control socket when it changes, and for a runtime with no traffic that is never:
an idle worker sends nothing and wakes for nothing.
.PP
Until the first such report has arrived,
.B stats
prints
.B worker_report:none
in place of those figures. The window is ordinarily the first fraction of a
second after a start; a runtime that shows it while serving traffic has a
worker that is alive but not publishing \(em wedged, or its frames being lost
\(em and that line is the difference between "no data" and "no traffic". Earlier
releases printed the placeholder's own untouched figures instead:
.B keys:0
from a store no command had ever reached, indistinguishable from a running
cache that nobody had used.
.PP
.B "And which process it is."
.B stats
prints
.BI pid: n
for a runtime that has one \(em an out\-of\-process proxy's worker, or a managed
external runtime's child. Nothing else in the output says the runtime is
elsewhere, so this is the line that names the process to strace, to read
.B /proc
for, or to account a machine's memory to. An in\-process runtime prints no such
line, and neither does one whose worker has been stopped or reaped \(em a pid
that has been returned to the system is worse than none, because it is a number
somebody will go and look at.
.PP
A sharded runtime prints
.BI shards: n
and
.B shard_pids:
instead, because there are
.I n
processes and naming one of them arbitrarily is the wrong answer. Its counters
are still one set of figures: the shared region has a line per shard and the sum
over them is what the runtime is serving.
.PP
Every type can be split, and every type is split unless it asks not to be. The
proxy was first, and the rest followed once they too routed from pushed data
rather than from a pointer into another runtime. What is left is written down in
this section: the cross\-runtime management API,
.BR self.route() ,
and figures that are pushed rather than read.
.SS TLS
.B "Every runtime type speaks TLS: a server's and a cache's own listener, a proxy \(em on both of its legs \(em and a client runtime's dial."
Created with
.BR "\-\-tls \-\-cert " file " \-\-key " file ,
a
.B server
accepts, handshakes and moves records itself. OpenSSL never touches the
socket: each connection gets a memory BIO pair, the ciphertext is carried in
and out by the same io_uring submissions as every other byte in the daemon,
and the handshake advances one completion at a time so nothing blocks the
event loop. Protocol detection, WebSocket, HTTP and RESP all run on the
decrypted stream, so
.B wss://
and
.B https://
work on a listener configured this way.
.BI \-\-ca " file"
adds mutual TLS \(em a client must present a certificate signed by that CA.
.PP
A peer that opens a connection and never finishes the handshake is dropped
after 30 seconds. Fixed, not configurable, and deliberately not
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout :
a connection that did handshake and then went quiet is legitimate on a
daemon built for long\-living ones, while a peer that has sent nothing the
record layer accepted has not become a client at all \(em and would
otherwise hold a slot against
.B \-\-max\-connections
for as long as it liked. The clock starts when the handshake does and later
bytes do not move it, so a peer that dribbles inside an unfinished record is
dropped on the same schedule as one that sends nothing at all. A
.B client
runtime applies the same 30 seconds to a target that accepts its connection
and then never handshakes, after which
.B \-\-reconnect
applies to it like any other lost connection.
.PP
A
.B proxy
runs the same record layer on both of its legs. The same three flags terminate
TLS on its listening port and
.BI \-\-client\-ca " file"
makes it demand a certificate from its callers; an
.B https://
backend address \(em or
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
or
.BR \-\-client\-key ,
which apply to every backend \(em takes the backend leg to TLS, with
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
presenting a certificate to a backend that asks for one. A TLS backend
connection is always verified, against
.B \-\-ca
or the system store, with the endpoint's name checked and TLS 1.2 as the
minimum version; there is no option to turn that off. Such connections are
pooled and reused, with their sessions, so a handshake is paid per idle period
rather than per request; a pooled socket is only ever returned to the endpoint
it was opened to, which is the name it was verified against.
.PP
.B "\-\-health\-check http"
works against a TLS backend and probes it over TLS. The probe runs the same
record layer the data path does \(em a memory BIO pair, records carried by
ordinary io_uring submissions, the handshake advanced one completion at a time
\(em and is held to the same standard: the chain against
.B \-\-ca
or the system store, the certificate against the endpoint's own name, TLS 1.2
minimum. A backend whose certificate does not verify is marked
.B unhealthy
rather than reported healthy, which is the only answer that agrees with what
the data path would do with it. Everything above the record layer is unchanged:
the same
.BI \-\-health\-path " path"
is fetched, and only a 2xx counts.
.PP
A
.B client
runtime runs the dialling half of that same record layer on its outbound
socket. Created with
.BR \-\-tls ,
it puts a ClientHello on the wire as soon as it has a socket and advances the
handshake one completion at a time, exactly as the other two do; the line
framing and the Lua callbacks above it see the same plaintext they always did.
The peer is verified against
.BI \-\-ca " file"
or the system store, and the certificate must be valid for the host in
.B \-t
\(em matched against its DNS SAN, or its IP SAN for an address literal.
.BI \-\-cert " file"
and
.BI \-\-key " file"
present a certificate to a peer that asks for one. There is no skip-verify
option, by design. A peer that fails either check costs the socket: the client
drops it and re-dials on its usual backoff, and never retries in cleartext.
.PP
A
.B cache
terminates TLS on its own listener with the same three flags a server uses, and
.BI \-\-ca " file"
demands a client certificate there in the same way. RESP is auto-detected from
the first byte of the decrypted stream, so an existing Redis client speaks the
protocol it always did, one layer up, and the line protocol works on the same
port.
.PP
.B "What a cache still cannot do over TLS is replicate."
Replication is not a listener and is not encrypted by anything: a follower
dials its leader itself, in cleartext, and on a leader a promoted follower is
written to by a second writer that goes around the record layer by design. So
the combination is refused rather than half-honoured \(em
.B \-\-tls
together with
.B \-\-replicate
is rejected at
.BR create ,
by
.BR edit " and " import ,
and again at every
.BR start ,
so a state file carrying both leaves the runtime
.B failed
rather than coming up quietly wrong; and a TLS cache asked to become a leader
by a
.B REPLICATE
command answers with an error instead of promoting itself. A cache that is
encrypted for its clients and cleartext for its replicas would be worse than
one that is honestly cleartext, because only the first of those looks safe.
.PP
Two further combinations are refused for the same reason:
.B \-\-udp
on a client or a server (DTLS is not implemented) and, on a server,
.B \-\-upstream
(the upstream connection would still be made in cleartext). A proxy in
.B \-\-protocol tcp
mode terminates TLS on its listener like any other, and forwards the decrypted
stream to its backend; the zero-copy splice path is bypassed on any connection
with a session, because a splice would move bytes the record layer never saw.
.PP
One outbound exception exists and is not transport:
.B socketley.http()
in a Lua callback speaks HTTPS, with the certificate chain and the host
name verified and TLS 1.2 as the minimum version (see
.BR "Global Functions" ).
That is a client call a script makes; it encrypts nothing that socketley
itself serves or forwards.
.PP
Earlier releases accepted the TLS flags on every runtime type, stored them,
persisted them, showed them in
.BR "socketley show" ,
and then encrypted nothing. A proxy created with
.B "\-\-tls \-\-cert \-\-key"
answered a plaintext HTTP request with 200 on the port it claimed to encrypt,
and a proxy aimed at an HTTPS backend sent a plaintext request line the
backend logged as a failed handshake. An operator asking for encryption was
told they had it. Both of those are now transport rather than decoration; the
roles that still have no handshake go on refusing the flags.
.PP
Everything that is not implemented therefore fails closed, and so does every
configuration that is incomplete:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-\-tls ", " \-\-cert ", " \-\-key " and " \-\-ca
on a
.B cache
runtime, and
.BR \-\-client\-ca ", " \-\-client\-cert " and " \-\-client\-key
on anything but a proxy, are rejected by
.B create
with exit status 1 and a message naming the flag.
.IP \(bu 2
on a
.BR server ", " client " or " proxy
runtime the same flags are accepted, but only as a complete set.
.B \-\-tls
without a certificate and key on a
.BR server " or " proxy ,
or a certificate or trust store without
.B \-\-tls
on any of the three, is refused and no runtime is created \(em either would
describe a listener that could only serve cleartext, or a dial that would be
made in it. On a
.B client
.B \-\-tls
alone is complete, but half a
.BR \-\-cert / \-\-key
pair is refused: it would present nothing while the flags said a certificate
was configured. On a proxy,
.B \-\-client\-ca
without
.B \-\-tls
and half a
.BR \-\-client\-cert / \-\-client\-key
pair are refused on the same reasoning.
.IP \(bu 2
flag-based
.B edit
applies the same rule, and restores the previous TLS settings if the edit
would leave them incomplete. It is a subset of
.BR create ,
so most flags it does not accept come back as
.BR "unknown flag" ;
these do not, because changing TLS on a live runtime is exactly what an
operator would be attempting.
.IP \(bu 2
.B import
applies the same rule to the JSON document's fields.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR start ", " reload
and the daemon's own boot-time restore refuse a runtime carrying a
configuration that cannot be honoured \(em which is how one restored from a
state file written by an older release is caught. Nothing is brought up; the
runtime goes to
.BR failed .
.IP \(bu 2
a certificate or key that cannot be read, or a key that does not match its
certificate, fails the runtime at start. The port is never bound.
.PP
To clear the settings from a runtime whose configuration is refused, dump it,
delete the TLS fields, and import it back (see
.BR import ).
.PP
The embedded C++ SDK reaches the same gate: its
.B start()
goes through
.BR runtime_manager::start ,
so
.B server().tls(cert, key)
and
.B proxy().tls(cert, key)
terminate TLS exactly as the daemon-managed runtime does, and the same call on
a client or cache fails the runtime instead of running in cleartext.
.PP
For encrypted transport on a
.BR client " or " cache
\(em or on a server's upstream connections \(em terminate TLS in a front-end
proxy (nginx, HAProxy, stunnel) and have it forward over loopback or an
otherwise trusted network path. A socketley
.B proxy
is now one of the things that can play that part.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error (invalid command, runtime not found, rejected TLS flag, etc.).
.TP
.B 2
Fatal error.
